1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,500
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:04,500
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:06,500
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,160
SARA BEERY: I hope everyone is having a good fall.

5
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:18,980
I don't know if any of all of you noticed,

6
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:22,580
but it started to be really cold today for the first time.

7
00:00:22,580 --> 00:00:27,880
And I went outside, and I was like, it begins.

8
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:32,680
So today, we're going to be continuing our series digging

9
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:34,600
into representation learning.

10
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:36,360
And specifically, I'm going to be

11
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:40,320
introducing the concept of similarity-based representation

12
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:41,360
learning.

13
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:46,160
So to start with a high-level roadmap,

14
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:48,720
today, we're going to be talking about why

15
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:51,520
we want to think about learning representations, why learning

16
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:56,960
representations is a good thing, try to get a sense of what makes

17
00:00:56,960 --> 00:01:00,030
a good representation both qualitatively

18
00:01:00,030 --> 00:01:02,930
but also exploring some dimensions of quantitatively

19
00:01:02,930 --> 00:01:05,050
what might make a good representation.

20
00:01:05,050 --> 00:01:08,330
We'll introduce the concept of metric learning.

21
00:01:08,330 --> 00:01:11,250
And then we'll dig into contrastive representation

22
00:01:11,250 --> 00:01:14,490
learning, which is essentially different creative ways

23
00:01:14,490 --> 00:01:18,130
to self-supervise the idea of building a metric space

24
00:01:18,130 --> 00:01:20,150
or using metric learning.

25
00:01:20,150 --> 00:01:22,470
And we'll talk about what contrastive learning does,

26
00:01:22,470 --> 00:01:24,090
and then some of the different potential models

27
00:01:24,090 --> 00:01:25,710
that are using contrastive learning.

28
00:01:25,710 --> 00:01:30,290
This is a pretty important point throughout this entire course

29
00:01:30,290 --> 00:01:33,170
because it turns out that many of our modern architectures

30
00:01:33,170 --> 00:01:36,370
are built on top of some form, or some component

31
00:01:36,370 --> 00:01:41,810
of contrastive learning and contrastive representations.

32
00:01:41,810 --> 00:01:46,610
So why learn representations?

33
00:01:46,610 --> 00:01:48,850
What are some reasons why we might

34
00:01:48,850 --> 00:01:51,190
want to learn a representation of data?

35
00:01:56,490 --> 00:01:57,320
Yeah, over there.

36
00:01:57,320 --> 00:01:58,820
AUDIENCE: Use it for any many tasks.

37
00:01:58,820 --> 00:02:01,340
SARA BEERY: We can use it for many tasks, the idea

38
00:02:01,340 --> 00:02:05,460
that maybe some representation of data is broadly useful.

39
00:02:05,460 --> 00:02:06,640
Anything else?

40
00:02:09,940 --> 00:02:10,580
Yeah?

41
00:02:10,580 --> 00:02:11,927
AUDIENCE: Maybe more compact.

42
00:02:11,927 --> 00:02:13,760
SARA BEERY: It might be more compact, right.

43
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:15,340
It might be more efficient to work

44
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:17,300
with some representation of data rather

45
00:02:17,300 --> 00:02:20,055
than the original data itself, though,

46
00:02:20,055 --> 00:02:21,180
of course, that's a choice.

47
00:02:21,180 --> 00:02:23,097
You can actually build a representation that's

48
00:02:23,097 --> 00:02:25,300
larger than your data input.

49
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:25,960
Anyone else?

50
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:28,260
What else makes it-- yeah, in the back?

51
00:02:28,260 --> 00:02:29,565
AUDIENCE: Generalization.

52
00:02:29,565 --> 00:02:31,440
SARA BEERY: To improve generalization, right.

53
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:35,660
We might want representations that actually are capturing

54
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:38,020
some semantic meaning or some important components

55
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:40,220
of the things we're trying to identify

56
00:02:40,220 --> 00:02:42,740
that will generalize well.

57
00:02:42,740 --> 00:02:45,733
So I mean, yes, to improve generalization--

58
00:02:45,733 --> 00:02:48,400
I'm going to trust you that you weren't just reading the slides,

59
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:51,700
but power to you if you were--

60
00:02:51,700 --> 00:02:54,400
to do more learning things, like transfer learning.

61
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:57,990
So often, we talk about this is that first point about working

62
00:02:57,990 --> 00:02:59,310
for many different tasks.

63
00:02:59,310 --> 00:03:02,710
We build some representation that then is more easily

64
00:03:02,710 --> 00:03:05,910
finetuned in a way that actually gives us something that's

65
00:03:05,910 --> 00:03:09,550
useful, maybe requiring less training data in that finetuning

66
00:03:09,550 --> 00:03:11,310
step.

67
00:03:11,310 --> 00:03:13,750
And then another version is actually maybe

68
00:03:13,750 --> 00:03:16,830
to exploit something like geometric similarity

69
00:03:16,830 --> 00:03:18,470
for new data or queries.

70
00:03:18,470 --> 00:03:20,530
So what does geometric similarity mean?

71
00:03:20,530 --> 00:03:24,190
Well, one thing might have we seen the face of this person

72
00:03:24,190 --> 00:03:26,050
before, or is it new?

73
00:03:26,050 --> 00:03:29,110
There's actually something physical and structural

74
00:03:29,110 --> 00:03:33,590
about our face that should be similar, regardless of maybe

75
00:03:33,590 --> 00:03:37,150
the lighting, the pose, the orientation.

76
00:03:37,150 --> 00:03:40,070
Or another version of something like geometric similarity

77
00:03:40,070 --> 00:03:42,270
might be in retrieval.

78
00:03:42,270 --> 00:03:43,750
Can you build representations that

79
00:03:43,750 --> 00:03:48,290
let you find items similar to a given query?

80
00:03:48,290 --> 00:03:51,410
So if I say, hey, here's something I just found,

81
00:03:51,410 --> 00:03:54,050
have we ever seen something similar to this before?

82
00:03:56,680 --> 00:04:00,600
Another example might be trying to do something improve

83
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:04,280
clustering with side information, something

84
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:08,920
about what makes pairs similar or dissimilar.

85
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:11,980
And then, possibly, we want to do dimensionality reduction--

86
00:04:11,980 --> 00:04:12,480
exactly.

87
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:14,800
Can we actually make our--

88
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:18,079
can we store this information in a much more compact way?

89
00:04:18,079 --> 00:04:20,360
And we can often do dimensionality reduction

90
00:04:20,360 --> 00:04:24,000
in ways that are fundamentally unsupervised.

91
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,800
So what are some of the things we might expect

92
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:28,180
from such representations?

93
00:04:28,180 --> 00:04:30,180
What makes a representation good?

94
00:04:35,320 --> 00:04:36,080
Yeah?

95
00:04:36,080 --> 00:04:40,240
AUDIENCE: It should map objects in your higher dimensional space

96
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:43,280
that are similar in semantic representation

97
00:04:43,280 --> 00:04:47,480
to the very close point in your embedding space.

98
00:04:47,480 --> 00:04:50,200
SARA BEERY: Yeah, so the idea that objects

99
00:04:50,200 --> 00:04:51,760
that are semantically similar, we

100
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:54,860
actually want them to be really close together

101
00:04:54,860 --> 00:04:56,660
in that representation space.

102
00:04:56,660 --> 00:04:58,647
We want them to be close together.

103
00:04:58,647 --> 00:05:00,480
Why would we want them to be close together?

104
00:05:00,480 --> 00:05:02,140
What does that do?

105
00:05:02,140 --> 00:05:03,580
Yeah?

106
00:05:03,580 --> 00:05:05,920
AUDIENCE: Helps us reject disturbances and noise,

107
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:06,803
potentially.

108
00:05:06,803 --> 00:05:08,220
SARA BEERY: Yeah, it might help us

109
00:05:08,220 --> 00:05:10,980
be robust to things like disturbances and noise

110
00:05:10,980 --> 00:05:12,900
if you move a little bit in any direction,

111
00:05:12,900 --> 00:05:15,940
if you're not moving of far away from things

112
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:18,380
that same semantic category.

113
00:05:18,380 --> 00:05:20,100
Why else might we want things that are

114
00:05:20,100 --> 00:05:22,340
similar to be close together?

115
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:23,660
Yeah, sure?

116
00:05:23,660 --> 00:05:25,360
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] the answer,

117
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:27,020
but one reason might be that if you

118
00:05:27,020 --> 00:05:30,620
think of a downstream function which takes the embeddings,

119
00:05:30,620 --> 00:05:33,500
but then logically, if I'm trying

120
00:05:33,500 --> 00:05:37,040
to compute some feature of two inputs which are very similar,

121
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:40,300
there's a good chance that I want f of x and f

122
00:05:40,300 --> 00:05:44,500
of x plus epsilon to have very similar y values.

123
00:05:44,500 --> 00:05:47,500
SARA BEERY: Yeah, so what you said was basically

124
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:50,620
we might want the downstream mapping of this embedding

125
00:05:50,620 --> 00:05:53,670
space to some maybe categorization

126
00:05:53,670 --> 00:05:56,190
to be kind of robust, to change, and to map things

127
00:05:56,190 --> 00:05:58,495
easily from one space to another.

128
00:05:58,495 --> 00:05:59,870
Actually, there's a different way

129
00:05:59,870 --> 00:06:03,750
to say that, which is basically it's useful if the space is

130
00:06:03,750 --> 00:06:05,070
linearly separable.

131
00:06:05,070 --> 00:06:07,190
It's a very easy function, if you

132
00:06:07,190 --> 00:06:10,085
want to learn categorization, if different objects that

133
00:06:10,085 --> 00:06:12,710
are the same are close together, and objects that are different

134
00:06:12,710 --> 00:06:14,670
are not close together because then you

135
00:06:14,670 --> 00:06:16,050
just draw lines between things.

136
00:06:16,050 --> 00:06:18,310
It's a very easy categorization problem.

137
00:06:18,310 --> 00:06:24,790
So a well-separated space is also potentially quite useful.

138
00:06:24,790 --> 00:06:29,550
So Goodfellow, back in 2016, said that, "generally speaking,

139
00:06:29,550 --> 00:06:33,150
a good representation is one that makes a subsequent learning

140
00:06:33,150 --> 00:06:34,410
task easier."

141
00:06:34,410 --> 00:06:36,590
That's exactly what you were trying to get at.

142
00:06:36,590 --> 00:06:38,150
And so what could that mean?

143
00:06:38,150 --> 00:06:40,030
What is a good representation?

144
00:06:40,030 --> 00:06:43,750
So one framing of this is explicitly

145
00:06:43,750 --> 00:06:45,750
what we were just trying to capture.

146
00:06:45,750 --> 00:06:48,710
A good representation is both compact,

147
00:06:48,710 --> 00:06:53,460
which means it's not too big.

148
00:06:53,460 --> 00:06:57,400
We don't want to have more capacity than we actually need.

149
00:06:57,400 --> 00:06:59,620
This is getting at that efficiency angle.

150
00:06:59,620 --> 00:07:02,640
You can take in information theoretic perspective

151
00:07:02,640 --> 00:07:04,920
here and think about we want to keep

152
00:07:04,920 --> 00:07:07,960
the minimal amount of information,

153
00:07:07,960 --> 00:07:11,600
but we also need it to be what we call explanatory.

154
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:14,880
So that means that it's somehow sufficient to capture

155
00:07:14,880 --> 00:07:18,320
the dimensions that matter in our original data.

156
00:07:18,320 --> 00:07:20,640
And the really interesting part here

157
00:07:20,640 --> 00:07:24,040
is that of course this is going to be contextually related

158
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:26,000
to the set of downstream tasks we might

159
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,240
be interested in because something that

160
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:31,400
is sufficient to be explanatory for one possible thing

161
00:07:31,400 --> 00:07:34,320
might be insufficient for another.

162
00:07:34,320 --> 00:07:37,280
And so a good representation, ideally,

163
00:07:37,280 --> 00:07:41,120
would be something that is kind of compact and explanatory

164
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:42,720
in a way that makes it generalize

165
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:45,640
to many possible downstream tasks.

166
00:07:45,640 --> 00:07:50,730
So back in 2020, there was this work

167
00:07:50,730 --> 00:07:54,010
that tried to pose specifically the question of not

168
00:07:54,010 --> 00:07:56,590
what makes a good categorizer, but actually,

169
00:07:56,590 --> 00:08:01,970
can we build complexity measures that accurately predict

170
00:08:01,970 --> 00:08:03,433
the generalization of models?

171
00:08:03,433 --> 00:08:04,850
Can you build a complexity measure

172
00:08:04,850 --> 00:08:07,290
over a representation that will predict

173
00:08:07,290 --> 00:08:11,610
how well that representation will generalize?

174
00:08:11,610 --> 00:08:15,010
And it turned out that a couple of winning strategies for this

175
00:08:15,010 --> 00:08:19,670
specifically looked at both the geometry of the representation,

176
00:08:19,670 --> 00:08:23,390
so that consistency and separation, how tight together,

177
00:08:23,390 --> 00:08:25,850
how close together were things that were the same, how

178
00:08:25,850 --> 00:08:28,130
far apart were things that were different,

179
00:08:28,130 --> 00:08:30,830
but also robustness to perturbations.

180
00:08:30,830 --> 00:08:34,370
So if you move a little bit in any dimension or any direction,

181
00:08:34,370 --> 00:08:37,049
do you actually change something fundamental

182
00:08:37,049 --> 00:08:39,409
about the representation?

183
00:08:39,409 --> 00:08:43,450
And this really maps quite well, I

184
00:08:43,450 --> 00:08:45,850
think, to some of our own intuition

185
00:08:45,850 --> 00:08:48,530
about what might make something easy to learn.

186
00:08:48,530 --> 00:08:51,060
And if you look at this specifically on something

187
00:08:51,060 --> 00:08:56,460
like CIFAR, CIFAR-10, which is a very low dimensional image

188
00:08:56,460 --> 00:09:00,340
categorization problem where all of the images are very, very low

189
00:09:00,340 --> 00:09:05,020
resolution and small, and the categories are simple.

190
00:09:05,020 --> 00:09:09,220
If you train a classifier on CIFAR-10 with the correct

191
00:09:09,220 --> 00:09:14,540
labels, and then you look at a t-SNE visualization

192
00:09:14,540 --> 00:09:16,608
of the representations on the training data--

193
00:09:16,608 --> 00:09:18,900
so this is the data that we've actually already trained

194
00:09:18,900 --> 00:09:20,100
the models on--

195
00:09:20,100 --> 00:09:22,100
what you see is it learns to build

196
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:28,320
a very consistent and separable training space.

197
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:30,723
The categories here are the same color.

198
00:09:30,723 --> 00:09:32,140
And so you can see the things that

199
00:09:32,140 --> 00:09:34,040
are the same color are very close together

200
00:09:34,040 --> 00:09:36,300
and they're very well-separated from everything else.

201
00:09:36,300 --> 00:09:38,540
And, interestingly, they're somewhat uniformly

202
00:09:38,540 --> 00:09:39,627
distributed in this space.

203
00:09:39,627 --> 00:09:41,460
And we'll actually talk later in the lecture

204
00:09:41,460 --> 00:09:44,740
about this other dimension of distribution

205
00:09:44,740 --> 00:09:46,860
throughout the space.

206
00:09:46,860 --> 00:09:52,590
Now, if you take the same model and you randomly

207
00:09:52,590 --> 00:09:54,750
generate the labels on that training data,

208
00:09:54,750 --> 00:09:57,990
and then you train a model to recognize what are now

209
00:09:57,990 --> 00:10:00,230
basically random noise, you still

210
00:10:00,230 --> 00:10:03,970
will get clusters because the model can learn to memorize.

211
00:10:03,970 --> 00:10:07,850
But what you see is that those clusters are much less concise.

212
00:10:07,850 --> 00:10:10,510
They're much less compact.

213
00:10:10,510 --> 00:10:13,550
The colors that are the same color

214
00:10:13,550 --> 00:10:15,910
are more spread out in the space.

215
00:10:15,910 --> 00:10:18,432
And they are much less separable.

216
00:10:18,432 --> 00:10:20,390
So you can imagine that this would be much less

217
00:10:20,390 --> 00:10:23,770
robust to perturbation because if you change in some direction,

218
00:10:23,770 --> 00:10:28,510
you're pretty quickly moving into another color, let's say.

219
00:10:28,510 --> 00:10:32,470
So this is really capturing that something

220
00:10:32,470 --> 00:10:35,430
that generalizes versus something that can't generalize

221
00:10:35,430 --> 00:10:37,890
will have this concentration or this consistency.

222
00:10:37,890 --> 00:10:39,990
Data from the same classes close together

223
00:10:39,990 --> 00:10:43,490
and separation classes are well-separated and robustness.

224
00:10:43,490 --> 00:10:45,570
If you move a little bit in any direction,

225
00:10:45,570 --> 00:10:47,280
you're not crossing what we might

226
00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:50,760
call a class boundary if you train a downstream classifier

227
00:10:50,760 --> 00:10:53,680
on this.

228
00:10:53,680 --> 00:10:57,360
So what makes a good representation?

229
00:10:57,360 --> 00:10:59,160
You want it to be compact.

230
00:10:59,160 --> 00:11:01,440
We want it to be explanatory.

231
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:04,600
We want to have concentration, data from the same classes

232
00:11:04,600 --> 00:11:05,500
close together.

233
00:11:05,500 --> 00:11:06,760
We want to have separation.

234
00:11:06,760 --> 00:11:08,400
Classes are well separated.

235
00:11:08,400 --> 00:11:12,500
And we want to have robustness to irrelevant perturbations,

236
00:11:12,500 --> 00:11:15,700
perturbations that we would not want to cross a class boundary.

237
00:11:15,700 --> 00:11:18,880
So a nice example of this-- and we'll get into this later--

238
00:11:18,880 --> 00:11:22,160
but if you saw me from this angle,

239
00:11:22,160 --> 00:11:25,860
you'd want to be robust to me looking the other direction.

240
00:11:25,860 --> 00:11:26,960
It's still Sarah.

241
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:29,340
It's not, somehow, a completely different class.

242
00:11:29,340 --> 00:11:32,560
So you might want to be robust to a perturbation that's

243
00:11:32,560 --> 00:11:34,760
something like pose.

244
00:11:34,760 --> 00:11:37,240
So how could we encourage a model

245
00:11:37,240 --> 00:11:39,520
during training to actually achieve

246
00:11:39,520 --> 00:11:43,360
this type of representation?

247
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:49,810
So one of the kind of high-level ideas

248
00:11:49,810 --> 00:11:53,190
that really has had a lot of traction

249
00:11:53,190 --> 00:11:55,490
here is the idea of explicitly encouraging

250
00:11:55,490 --> 00:11:58,610
good representations via feedback in terms

251
00:11:58,610 --> 00:12:01,610
of the similarity of data.

252
00:12:01,610 --> 00:12:04,670
So you have pairs of either similar or dissimilar inputs.

253
00:12:04,670 --> 00:12:07,130
You're not just looking at one example at a time.

254
00:12:07,130 --> 00:12:10,010
You're actually looking at two or maybe three examples

255
00:12:10,010 --> 00:12:11,810
and giving information to the model

256
00:12:11,810 --> 00:12:15,130
about what should be similar and what should be dissimilar.

257
00:12:15,130 --> 00:12:17,090
So why might this be different than just

258
00:12:17,090 --> 00:12:18,470
looking at one image at a time?

259
00:12:18,470 --> 00:12:20,470
So there's actually a really nice example,

260
00:12:20,470 --> 00:12:23,250
I think, from psychology about the value

261
00:12:23,250 --> 00:12:26,370
of this contextualization or this comparison.

262
00:12:26,370 --> 00:12:30,050
So tell me what an elephant looks like.

263
00:12:30,050 --> 00:12:32,010
Describe an elephant to me.

264
00:12:32,010 --> 00:12:37,807
AUDIENCE: Very big, very bulky, and huge-sized.

265
00:12:37,807 --> 00:12:39,890
SARA BEERY: OK, very big and bulky and huge-sized.

266
00:12:39,890 --> 00:12:40,930
AUDIENCE: Large ears.

267
00:12:40,930 --> 00:12:43,330
SARA BEERY: Large ears, OK.

268
00:12:43,330 --> 00:12:45,860
Anything else?

269
00:12:45,860 --> 00:12:47,680
AUDIENCE: I think it has also--

270
00:12:50,420 --> 00:12:53,120
yeah, I think I'd also like something in the front of it.

271
00:12:53,120 --> 00:12:57,640
SARA BEERY: Like some big nose, trunk.

272
00:12:57,640 --> 00:13:01,820
So now if you knew nothing about elephants,

273
00:13:01,820 --> 00:13:04,740
you could draw almost an infinite set

274
00:13:04,740 --> 00:13:08,300
of things that might have those characteristics.

275
00:13:08,300 --> 00:13:12,400
And probably none of them would look very close to an elephant.

276
00:13:12,400 --> 00:13:17,580
So now you, in the front row, or in the second row,

277
00:13:17,580 --> 00:13:22,660
you, pretend I already know what a rhinoceros looks like.

278
00:13:22,660 --> 00:13:25,720
Now try to describe what an elephant looks like to me.

279
00:13:30,420 --> 00:13:32,348
AUDIENCE: Instead of a trunk, it has a horn.

280
00:13:32,348 --> 00:13:34,640
SARA BEERY: OK, so I know what a rhinoceros looks like.

281
00:13:34,640 --> 00:13:36,540
So now you're saying, OK, instead

282
00:13:36,540 --> 00:13:38,840
of having a horn in the front, take a rhinoceros,

283
00:13:38,840 --> 00:13:44,230
but add a long trunk, and then maybe add some big ears.

284
00:13:44,230 --> 00:13:47,190
I'm actually probably much more likely to have an understanding

285
00:13:47,190 --> 00:13:50,750
of what that thing is because I understand similarity and then

286
00:13:50,750 --> 00:13:52,870
these small contrastive differences.

287
00:13:52,870 --> 00:13:55,790
And I actually think this is a really nice, intuitive example

288
00:13:55,790 --> 00:13:58,710
of how we think about contrastive learning

289
00:13:58,710 --> 00:14:01,270
and why this type of contrastive signal is so valuable.

290
00:14:01,270 --> 00:14:03,510
And I actually think, interestingly enough, I

291
00:14:03,510 --> 00:14:05,030
work a lot with species.

292
00:14:05,030 --> 00:14:07,990
One of the ways that we train people in identifying species

293
00:14:07,990 --> 00:14:10,190
is almost always contrastively.

294
00:14:10,190 --> 00:14:12,710
If I'm teaching you how to identify two different types

295
00:14:12,710 --> 00:14:15,490
of birds, I'm not going to say, oh,

296
00:14:15,490 --> 00:14:17,510
I'm going to describe this one from scratch

297
00:14:17,510 --> 00:14:20,570
with this massive, exhaustive list of independent attributes.

298
00:14:20,570 --> 00:14:23,290
I'll say it looks a lot like this other bird,

299
00:14:23,290 --> 00:14:26,070
but the important differences are the spot near the eye

300
00:14:26,070 --> 00:14:27,490
and the color of the legs.

301
00:14:27,490 --> 00:14:32,110
It's this comparative difference.

302
00:14:32,110 --> 00:14:36,830
So the way that we actually try to train things

303
00:14:36,830 --> 00:14:39,950
based on similarity and dissimilarity,

304
00:14:39,950 --> 00:14:43,050
the original proposed approach was this thing

305
00:14:43,050 --> 00:14:45,050
called metric learning.

306
00:14:45,050 --> 00:14:47,450
And essentially, this was based on the idea

307
00:14:47,450 --> 00:14:50,090
that measuring Euclidean distance in your input data

308
00:14:50,090 --> 00:14:50,590
space.

309
00:14:50,590 --> 00:14:52,570
So for example, looking at Euclidean distance

310
00:14:52,570 --> 00:14:55,050
between all the pixels in one image and another

311
00:14:55,050 --> 00:14:57,130
might not be ideal.

312
00:14:57,130 --> 00:15:00,970
Instead, we might want to learn some metric, some representation

313
00:15:00,970 --> 00:15:03,810
that respects some desired properties.

314
00:15:03,810 --> 00:15:06,450
And so our goal is to learn a metric where data points that

315
00:15:06,450 --> 00:15:08,713
belong together are similar, close together,

316
00:15:08,713 --> 00:15:10,130
and data points that are different

317
00:15:10,130 --> 00:15:12,050
are dissimilar far apart.

318
00:15:12,050 --> 00:15:15,290
And then the supervision becomes similarity information.

319
00:15:15,290 --> 00:15:19,970
So for example, maybe you'd have three images

320
00:15:19,970 --> 00:15:23,850
of your instructors, and you would want to explicitly say

321
00:15:23,850 --> 00:15:26,210
that the two images of Phil should

322
00:15:26,210 --> 00:15:28,490
be closer together because they're both Phil,

323
00:15:28,490 --> 00:15:33,650
and the image of me would be far apart because I'm not Phil.

324
00:15:33,650 --> 00:15:36,370
And so, actually, how do we do this?

325
00:15:36,370 --> 00:15:38,810
So the very, very simple version,

326
00:15:38,810 --> 00:15:42,740
which is just in one-dimensional space, let's assume.

327
00:15:42,740 --> 00:15:46,060
Say you have some data points x1 through xn,

328
00:15:46,060 --> 00:15:49,340
and you have some form of supervision.

329
00:15:49,340 --> 00:15:51,220
And this supervision is not exactly

330
00:15:51,220 --> 00:15:54,060
the distances between things or how similar or dissimilar

331
00:15:54,060 --> 00:15:54,860
there are.

332
00:15:54,860 --> 00:15:56,660
We just have supervision in terms

333
00:15:56,660 --> 00:15:59,300
of which pairs are in the same category

334
00:15:59,300 --> 00:16:01,900
and which pairs are in different categories.

335
00:16:01,900 --> 00:16:03,900
So we have some set of similar pairs

336
00:16:03,900 --> 00:16:05,980
and some set of dissimilar pairs.

337
00:16:05,980 --> 00:16:08,580
And our goal is to learn a linear transformation,

338
00:16:08,580 --> 00:16:10,740
z equals Wx.

339
00:16:10,740 --> 00:16:14,300
So essentially, we're trying to learn this matrix W that

340
00:16:14,300 --> 00:16:17,500
will respect this similarity.

341
00:16:17,500 --> 00:16:20,340
And so the idea here is that we can use Euclidean distance

342
00:16:20,340 --> 00:16:22,620
in representation space.

343
00:16:22,620 --> 00:16:25,780
And so now if we map out, OK, we want

344
00:16:25,780 --> 00:16:30,340
the distance between zi and xj to be equal to,

345
00:16:30,340 --> 00:16:34,820
factored out, the difference in terms of xi and xj

346
00:16:34,820 --> 00:16:38,600
transpose W transpose W the difference between xi and xj.

347
00:16:38,600 --> 00:16:41,390
We're were just taking that norm and factoring it out.

348
00:16:41,390 --> 00:16:46,990
Then you can define, basically, this A norm on xi and xj

349
00:16:46,990 --> 00:16:51,590
that will be W transpose W, which is a positive semidefinite

350
00:16:51,590 --> 00:16:52,790
matrix.

351
00:16:52,790 --> 00:16:54,830
We like positive semidefinite matrices

352
00:16:54,830 --> 00:16:57,630
because they're easy to work with mathematically.

353
00:16:57,630 --> 00:17:00,790
And so this distance here actually

354
00:17:00,790 --> 00:17:04,710
is explicitly defined as the Mahalanobis distance.

355
00:17:04,710 --> 00:17:08,390
And so the Mahalanobis distance between two points

356
00:17:08,390 --> 00:17:12,170
is going to be defined by positive semidefinite matrix A.

357
00:17:12,170 --> 00:17:15,369
And it's defined explicitly as that.

358
00:17:15,369 --> 00:17:18,990
And so what you can show is that this,

359
00:17:18,990 --> 00:17:22,790
if we can learn some of optimal A, which essentially would

360
00:17:22,790 --> 00:17:26,470
be an optimal W for this z equals Wx,

361
00:17:26,470 --> 00:17:29,910
then that will actually be this transformation that

362
00:17:29,910 --> 00:17:33,110
preserves similarity and difference maximally.

363
00:17:33,110 --> 00:17:36,670
So how could we actually phrase that as an optimization problem?

364
00:17:36,670 --> 00:17:40,060
How do we learn the right W here?

365
00:17:43,120 --> 00:17:44,140
Any ideas?

366
00:17:51,560 --> 00:17:56,520
So essentially, the first proposed approach,

367
00:17:56,520 --> 00:17:59,880
and one that was really introduced in the paper that

368
00:17:59,880 --> 00:18:02,320
defined the term metric learning, and they call

369
00:18:02,320 --> 00:18:06,800
it distance metric learning, was to specifically set this up

370
00:18:06,800 --> 00:18:10,200
as an optimization problem with a constraint.

371
00:18:10,200 --> 00:18:13,360
So here, what we can do is we can

372
00:18:13,360 --> 00:18:17,420
say we want to find A. We want to find positive semidefinite

373
00:18:17,420 --> 00:18:21,702
A such that the minimum distance of similar--

374
00:18:21,702 --> 00:18:24,160
we want to minimize the distance between similar points, so

375
00:18:24,160 --> 00:18:26,760
between those pairs that are known to be similar,

376
00:18:26,760 --> 00:18:32,000
while making sure that there is at least a distance of 1

377
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:36,988
or some epsilon between dissimilar points.

378
00:18:36,988 --> 00:18:38,530
So you're essentially setting this up

379
00:18:38,530 --> 00:18:39,770
as an optimization problem, where

380
00:18:39,770 --> 00:18:41,853
you're trying to bring the things that are similar

381
00:18:41,853 --> 00:18:44,730
as close together as possible, while enforcing that there is

382
00:18:44,730 --> 00:18:49,450
at least some margin around any point and any other point

383
00:18:49,450 --> 00:18:51,005
that is dissimilar to it.

384
00:18:51,005 --> 00:18:52,630
And you can actually flip this as well.

385
00:18:52,630 --> 00:18:55,370
You can also think of it as keeping

386
00:18:55,370 --> 00:18:58,690
the distance between similar points within some constraint,

387
00:18:58,690 --> 00:19:03,250
and maximizing the difference between dissimilar points.

388
00:19:03,250 --> 00:19:06,690
And there's now a lot of different variations

389
00:19:06,690 --> 00:19:10,130
of this that have been proposed since 2003,

390
00:19:10,130 --> 00:19:13,650
which is now over 20 years ago.

391
00:19:13,650 --> 00:19:17,690
And it really is something that can be explicitly solvable.

392
00:19:17,690 --> 00:19:21,585
And you can, again, swap the objective and constraint.

393
00:19:21,585 --> 00:19:24,210
And there's actually been a lot of related ideas and follow-ups

394
00:19:24,210 --> 00:19:26,710
to this, things like, for example,

395
00:19:26,710 --> 00:19:28,810
information theoretic metric learning,

396
00:19:28,810 --> 00:19:31,970
which explicitly proposed preserving distribution

397
00:19:31,970 --> 00:19:33,210
information.

398
00:19:33,210 --> 00:19:36,380
So for example, relative entropy between Gaussians,

399
00:19:36,380 --> 00:19:38,620
while trying to observe both upper and lower

400
00:19:38,620 --> 00:19:40,580
bounds as constraints.

401
00:19:40,580 --> 00:19:43,220
But a really simple example of what

402
00:19:43,220 --> 00:19:45,180
this is, like why you might want to learn

403
00:19:45,180 --> 00:19:49,300
some projection, some linear projection that

404
00:19:49,300 --> 00:19:52,120
preserves these similarities and dissimilarities, is this one.

405
00:19:52,120 --> 00:19:53,860
I think this is incredibly intuitive.

406
00:19:53,860 --> 00:19:58,720
So say your original data was bimodal in some way.

407
00:19:58,720 --> 00:20:03,713
There's clearly red and blue categories.

408
00:20:03,713 --> 00:20:05,380
And then there's one mode, where there's

409
00:20:05,380 --> 00:20:06,797
red and blue categories over here,

410
00:20:06,797 --> 00:20:08,460
and one where they're over here.

411
00:20:08,460 --> 00:20:11,500
The very, very simple optimal projection

412
00:20:11,500 --> 00:20:14,380
here would be just removing the dimension.

413
00:20:14,380 --> 00:20:17,960
Here, it would be like along the y-axis.

414
00:20:17,960 --> 00:20:21,000
So now you're just projecting everything into the same plane.

415
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:26,480
And now they're very easily separable from that perspective.

416
00:20:29,718 --> 00:20:32,260
There's a lot of improvements and developments on top of this

417
00:20:32,260 --> 00:20:33,790
that you can do.

418
00:20:33,790 --> 00:20:39,790
So one thing is you can think about looking

419
00:20:39,790 --> 00:20:41,910
at nonlinear transformations.

420
00:20:41,910 --> 00:20:45,990
So instead of only learning a linear projection W,

421
00:20:45,990 --> 00:20:49,710
you can think about doing things like deep metric learning, where

422
00:20:49,710 --> 00:20:53,350
you're actually explicitly learning some of neural network

423
00:20:53,350 --> 00:20:57,070
that would map into a space that keeps things similar that we

424
00:20:57,070 --> 00:20:59,867
want similar and dissimilar that we want dissimilar.

425
00:20:59,867 --> 00:21:01,950
We've introduced these concepts of different types

426
00:21:01,950 --> 00:21:03,250
of contrastive losses.

427
00:21:03,250 --> 00:21:05,110
We'll get into those a little bit more.

428
00:21:05,110 --> 00:21:07,470
And then another improvement and something

429
00:21:07,470 --> 00:21:10,510
that actually is just tracked to be quite helpful

430
00:21:10,510 --> 00:21:13,090
is the idea of normalizing your representations.

431
00:21:13,090 --> 00:21:15,530
So instead of then taking Euclidean distance,

432
00:21:15,530 --> 00:21:17,630
if you normalize representations to enforce

433
00:21:17,630 --> 00:21:21,910
that all of the distances have to live on the hypersphere,

434
00:21:21,910 --> 00:21:24,590
then distance would be essentially just

435
00:21:24,590 --> 00:21:25,965
equivalent to angle.

436
00:21:25,965 --> 00:21:27,590
And then you can look at inner products

437
00:21:27,590 --> 00:21:29,310
to measure similarity instead of needing

438
00:21:29,310 --> 00:21:30,930
to look at Euclidean distance.

439
00:21:30,930 --> 00:21:32,640
And this is nice because you don't end up

440
00:21:32,640 --> 00:21:35,460
having things that get really blown up by scale.

441
00:21:35,460 --> 00:21:38,060
You're removing the scale complexity.

442
00:21:41,320 --> 00:21:45,655
So this deep metric learning idea is quite simple.

443
00:21:45,655 --> 00:21:47,280
Linear metric learning is you're trying

444
00:21:47,280 --> 00:21:50,458
to learn some linear transformation z equals Wx.

445
00:21:50,458 --> 00:21:52,000
Deep metric learning is you're trying

446
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,720
to learn a nonlinear transformation z

447
00:21:54,720 --> 00:21:58,080
equals f of x, where now f of x is a neural network.

448
00:21:58,080 --> 00:22:00,320
And now you're not optimizing over the set

449
00:22:00,320 --> 00:22:02,088
of positive semidefinite matrices.

450
00:22:02,088 --> 00:22:04,380
You're optimizing over the weights of a neural network.

451
00:22:04,380 --> 00:22:07,040
And there, we can use things like stochastic gradient descent

452
00:22:07,040 --> 00:22:09,280
to do so.

453
00:22:09,280 --> 00:22:11,400
So the intuition behind this introduction

454
00:22:11,400 --> 00:22:16,120
of contrastive losses is the following.

455
00:22:16,120 --> 00:22:19,000
So say I show you this moth.

456
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:22,000
What kind of moth is that?

457
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,840
Anybody really into moths in this room?

458
00:22:24,840 --> 00:22:27,240
I'm guessing no.

459
00:22:27,240 --> 00:22:30,500
Lepidoptera is the scientific term for the genus.

460
00:22:30,500 --> 00:22:33,450
Anyway, so that's a moth.

461
00:22:33,450 --> 00:22:36,795
I actually also don't know what kind of moth that is.

462
00:22:36,795 --> 00:22:37,670
There's another moth.

463
00:22:37,670 --> 00:22:38,810
Are these the same moth?

464
00:22:38,810 --> 00:22:41,668
Are they the same kind of moth?

465
00:22:41,668 --> 00:22:42,210
AUDIENCE: No.

466
00:22:45,210 --> 00:22:47,730
SARA BEERY: It's kind of hard to say, right?

467
00:22:47,730 --> 00:22:50,110
Well, OK, what if I showed you another moth?

468
00:22:50,110 --> 00:22:53,850
What if I showed you this moth?

469
00:22:53,850 --> 00:22:56,170
If you had to guess, and you had to say two of these

470
00:22:56,170 --> 00:22:59,530
were the same kind of moth, which two would you guess?

471
00:22:59,530 --> 00:23:00,950
Yeah, right.

472
00:23:00,950 --> 00:23:04,090
So it might be really hard to explicitly say whether something

473
00:23:04,090 --> 00:23:06,050
is the same or different, especially

474
00:23:06,050 --> 00:23:09,370
because when you get into things like this, actually,

475
00:23:09,370 --> 00:23:13,650
there's this dimension of what is a category that

476
00:23:13,650 --> 00:23:14,790
gets complicated.

477
00:23:14,790 --> 00:23:16,762
Is the category all moths?

478
00:23:16,762 --> 00:23:17,970
Well, then, they're the same.

479
00:23:17,970 --> 00:23:19,272
Is the category white moths?

480
00:23:19,272 --> 00:23:20,230
They're still the same.

481
00:23:20,230 --> 00:23:22,505
Is it the category white moths on green leaves?

482
00:23:22,505 --> 00:23:23,130
Still the same.

483
00:23:23,130 --> 00:23:25,850
Is the category species of moth?

484
00:23:25,850 --> 00:23:30,020
Then maybe we don't know anymore.

485
00:23:30,020 --> 00:23:32,980
But it's actually very easy, regardless of what

486
00:23:32,980 --> 00:23:37,300
you want your downstream task and the dimension of granularity

487
00:23:37,300 --> 00:23:40,820
to be to say those two are definitely

488
00:23:40,820 --> 00:23:43,660
more similar than that one.

489
00:23:43,660 --> 00:23:46,220
So the way we translate this into machine learning

490
00:23:46,220 --> 00:23:48,580
is we try to build a loss that explicitly

491
00:23:48,580 --> 00:23:51,140
says that the distance between dissimilar pairs

492
00:23:51,140 --> 00:23:56,060
must be much larger than the distance between similar pairs.

493
00:23:56,060 --> 00:23:58,540
And the very initial version that was introduced here

494
00:23:58,540 --> 00:24:01,580
is this idea of what's called triplet loss, which essentially

495
00:24:01,580 --> 00:24:04,200
demands that the distance between an anchor.

496
00:24:04,200 --> 00:24:06,820
So you have one point that you say is your anchor point,

497
00:24:06,820 --> 00:24:10,020
and then you have two other data points that are sent in too.

498
00:24:10,020 --> 00:24:12,620
And one of them is a positive example

499
00:24:12,620 --> 00:24:14,195
of the same as that anchor point.

500
00:24:14,195 --> 00:24:15,820
And the other one is a negative example

501
00:24:15,820 --> 00:24:17,680
that's different from that anchor point.

502
00:24:17,680 --> 00:24:20,780
Now you enforce that the distance between the anchor

503
00:24:20,780 --> 00:24:23,740
and the negative example has to be bigger

504
00:24:23,740 --> 00:24:26,400
than the distance between the anchor and the positive example,

505
00:24:26,400 --> 00:24:30,280
plus some margin-- again, this kind of enforced margin.

506
00:24:30,280 --> 00:24:33,200
And so what this actually looks like in practice

507
00:24:33,200 --> 00:24:36,200
is you're just explicitly enforcing

508
00:24:36,200 --> 00:24:39,000
that you want to have some loss that penalizes

509
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:42,560
if that condition is not met.

510
00:24:42,560 --> 00:24:45,700
And so then, when you actually look at data examples here,

511
00:24:45,700 --> 00:24:51,120
so maybe you take in your initial data, and in that space,

512
00:24:51,120 --> 00:24:54,160
it turns out that, incorrectly, your model

513
00:24:54,160 --> 00:24:58,600
had put this first horse, the anchor horse x, and this monkey

514
00:24:58,600 --> 00:25:02,640
closer together than the anchor horse and this other horse.

515
00:25:02,640 --> 00:25:05,220
And now, after actually updating your model,

516
00:25:05,220 --> 00:25:07,760
taking a gradient descent step, the loss and that gradient

517
00:25:07,760 --> 00:25:10,440
would tell you that you needed to push, in that representation

518
00:25:10,440 --> 00:25:13,080
space, the horse and the monkey further

519
00:25:13,080 --> 00:25:17,600
apart and the horse and the other horse closer together.

520
00:25:17,600 --> 00:25:21,480
And the way this actually is done is in a triplet network.

521
00:25:21,480 --> 00:25:23,720
You would have these shared weights.

522
00:25:23,720 --> 00:25:25,700
So you have three images that go in.

523
00:25:25,700 --> 00:25:28,250
You run all three of them through the same convolutional

524
00:25:28,250 --> 00:25:29,410
neural network.

525
00:25:29,410 --> 00:25:31,970
You calculate embeddings for all three.

526
00:25:31,970 --> 00:25:35,170
You take a triplet loss over those three different embeddings

527
00:25:35,170 --> 00:25:36,810
based on the anchor, the positive,

528
00:25:36,810 --> 00:25:38,490
and the negative example, and then

529
00:25:38,490 --> 00:25:44,210
you back propagate through that branch in the graph.

530
00:25:44,210 --> 00:25:47,610
And so there are actually quite a few different improvements

531
00:25:47,610 --> 00:25:48,930
of this.

532
00:25:48,930 --> 00:25:51,090
One is actually pretty explicitly related

533
00:25:51,090 --> 00:25:53,010
to training efficiency.

534
00:25:53,010 --> 00:25:55,690
And that's just that you can now,

535
00:25:55,690 --> 00:25:59,730
instead of sending in all of these independent triplets,

536
00:25:59,730 --> 00:26:01,610
you can load an entire batch.

537
00:26:01,610 --> 00:26:03,410
And now you'll have some positive examples

538
00:26:03,410 --> 00:26:05,490
and some negative examples in that batch.

539
00:26:05,490 --> 00:26:08,330
And then you can construct every possible triplet

540
00:26:08,330 --> 00:26:13,082
of positive and negative and calculate the loss over those.

541
00:26:13,082 --> 00:26:15,290
And so that way you're actually getting more training

542
00:26:15,290 --> 00:26:15,830
efficiency.

543
00:26:15,830 --> 00:26:17,530
You're making the best use of the batch you've already

544
00:26:17,530 --> 00:26:18,510
loaded in.

545
00:26:18,510 --> 00:26:21,390
And also, in this way, you're more likely to sample,

546
00:26:21,390 --> 00:26:24,730
for example, these hard negatives that give you a signal

547
00:26:24,730 --> 00:26:27,060
because, for example, if you send

548
00:26:27,060 --> 00:26:30,140
in an image or a set of three images

549
00:26:30,140 --> 00:26:33,580
and your representation already captures well

550
00:26:33,580 --> 00:26:35,940
that these two are--

551
00:26:35,940 --> 00:26:37,620
the positive example is closer than

552
00:26:37,620 --> 00:26:40,020
the negative example with a margin,

553
00:26:40,020 --> 00:26:41,880
then you don't get any signal.

554
00:26:41,880 --> 00:26:43,380
So the easy stuff doesn't actually

555
00:26:43,380 --> 00:26:47,040
help you train because you don't have any signal saying,

556
00:26:47,040 --> 00:26:48,280
ah, no, you got that wrong.

557
00:26:48,280 --> 00:26:49,600
You need to move them apart.

558
00:26:49,600 --> 00:26:51,980
So you really need to see these hard examples, where

559
00:26:51,980 --> 00:26:54,600
your model is getting it wrong, to be able to get signal

560
00:26:54,600 --> 00:26:57,267
and to be able to actually get a gradient that trains your model

561
00:26:57,267 --> 00:26:59,660
to improve your representation.

562
00:26:59,660 --> 00:27:03,900
So if you use this type of embedding, what you can see

563
00:27:03,900 --> 00:27:06,460
is get clusters.

564
00:27:06,460 --> 00:27:08,420
You have done this in a way where

565
00:27:08,420 --> 00:27:11,780
now things that are similar tend to be clustered close together.

566
00:27:11,780 --> 00:27:14,660
In this case, this is a triplet loss network

567
00:27:14,660 --> 00:27:18,660
that was trained over the Caltech UCSD Birds data set.

568
00:27:18,660 --> 00:27:21,380
And you can see that, in this embedding space,

569
00:27:21,380 --> 00:27:24,550
things that are close together are actually quite similar.

570
00:27:24,550 --> 00:27:27,230
Are there some other things that this thing has learned

571
00:27:27,230 --> 00:27:29,130
beyond maybe similar species?

572
00:27:32,750 --> 00:27:33,330
Yeah?

573
00:27:33,330 --> 00:27:34,247
AUDIENCE: Environment.

574
00:27:34,247 --> 00:27:35,557
SARA BEERY: Environment, yeah.

575
00:27:35,557 --> 00:27:37,890
So if you look at, for example, that cluster over there,

576
00:27:37,890 --> 00:27:40,710
it's a cluster where, yes, all of the examples of those birds

577
00:27:40,710 --> 00:27:43,558
are terns, the ones that are flying against a blue sky.

578
00:27:43,558 --> 00:27:44,850
I could tell you they're terns.

579
00:27:44,850 --> 00:27:47,030
Maybe some of you guys know they're terns.

580
00:27:47,030 --> 00:27:50,550
But they're also all pretty much birds that are flying

581
00:27:50,550 --> 00:27:53,390
and mostly birds that are flying against a blue sky.

582
00:27:53,390 --> 00:27:55,630
So you can imagine that similarity here

583
00:27:55,630 --> 00:27:58,590
has may be picked up on some more stuff than just maybe what

584
00:27:58,590 --> 00:28:02,310
we might be interested in terms of similarity.

585
00:28:02,310 --> 00:28:05,250
And if you query using this, you say,

586
00:28:05,250 --> 00:28:09,390
OK, find me nearest neighbors in this embedding space,

587
00:28:09,390 --> 00:28:14,470
you also do start to learn similarity semantically.

588
00:28:14,470 --> 00:28:19,150
You start to learn that things that look similar are similar

589
00:28:19,150 --> 00:28:22,350
and things that look different are far apart.

590
00:28:22,350 --> 00:28:24,280
And this gets at this interesting question

591
00:28:24,280 --> 00:28:27,820
of what makes an image similar.

592
00:28:27,820 --> 00:28:31,280
So here, these are embedding spaces

593
00:28:31,280 --> 00:28:34,760
that are trained on much larger data sets.

594
00:28:34,760 --> 00:28:36,740
And we have some reference image.

595
00:28:36,740 --> 00:28:40,040
And then we have different things

596
00:28:40,040 --> 00:28:44,120
that are kind of coming in that are potentially

597
00:28:44,120 --> 00:28:45,580
similar or dissimilar.

598
00:28:45,580 --> 00:28:48,880
And this was a paper, actually, of Phil's, where they explicitly

599
00:28:48,880 --> 00:28:53,560
asked humans what they thought was the most similar, trying

600
00:28:53,560 --> 00:28:56,240
to get this sense of how does a human brain think

601
00:28:56,240 --> 00:28:57,198
about similarity.

602
00:28:57,198 --> 00:28:59,240
And it turns out, there's a lot of different ways

603
00:28:59,240 --> 00:29:00,540
that we think about similarity.

604
00:29:00,540 --> 00:29:04,520
We might think about similarity in pose, perspective, foreground

605
00:29:04,520 --> 00:29:07,800
color, foreground-background orientation, the number

606
00:29:07,800 --> 00:29:09,720
of items, object shape.

607
00:29:09,720 --> 00:29:12,320
There's a lot of different ways that things can be similar.

608
00:29:12,320 --> 00:29:16,160
And what's the most useful way to have things

609
00:29:16,160 --> 00:29:18,960
be similar or different based on a representation space

610
00:29:18,960 --> 00:29:20,600
is probably going to be contextualized

611
00:29:20,600 --> 00:29:25,450
by some downstream task that we're interested in.

612
00:29:25,450 --> 00:29:28,410
So how do we actually try to make sure

613
00:29:28,410 --> 00:29:33,450
that we're showing these images that are hard to the models

614
00:29:33,450 --> 00:29:35,610
that we get training signal?

615
00:29:35,610 --> 00:29:40,290
So we need to find basically hard negatives.

616
00:29:40,290 --> 00:29:42,250
And this type of hard negative mining

617
00:29:42,250 --> 00:29:46,030
will often look for a misplaced negative.

618
00:29:46,030 --> 00:29:48,090
So we need to try to find things that

619
00:29:48,090 --> 00:29:51,850
have been put closer to the anchor than a positive example.

620
00:29:51,850 --> 00:29:54,050
And being able to do this hard negative mining

621
00:29:54,050 --> 00:29:56,370
helps us accelerate our learning.

622
00:29:56,370 --> 00:29:58,430
The more we can find the hard things,

623
00:29:58,430 --> 00:30:00,850
the more we train quickly.

624
00:30:00,850 --> 00:30:02,710
And one nice example of that is this.

625
00:30:02,710 --> 00:30:04,390
So here's our moths again.

626
00:30:04,390 --> 00:30:07,890
You can imagine that if you saw those first two images based

627
00:30:07,890 --> 00:30:10,290
on a lot of dimensions of similarity,

628
00:30:10,290 --> 00:30:15,170
like the pose, the background color, the orientation,

629
00:30:15,170 --> 00:30:18,130
these are actually more similar than this one on the bottom,

630
00:30:18,130 --> 00:30:20,680
but in some of semantic categorization space,

631
00:30:20,680 --> 00:30:22,548
we want to know the type of moth.

632
00:30:22,548 --> 00:30:24,340
These ones should actually be more similar,

633
00:30:24,340 --> 00:30:25,940
and that one should be further away.

634
00:30:25,940 --> 00:30:28,460
And those things like pose and background color

635
00:30:28,460 --> 00:30:31,380
are actually confounding dimensions right there.

636
00:30:31,380 --> 00:30:34,380
There are ways that we want our model to be invariant.

637
00:30:34,380 --> 00:30:36,220
And so finding examples like this

638
00:30:36,220 --> 00:30:38,700
would be explicitly ways to train your model faster

639
00:30:38,700 --> 00:30:43,580
to recognize what you want to be similar.

640
00:30:43,580 --> 00:30:49,420
So this assumes, though, that we already know the right answer.

641
00:30:49,420 --> 00:30:52,420
It assumes that we know that these two moths at the bottom

642
00:30:52,420 --> 00:30:55,740
are the same and then that one at the top is different.

643
00:30:55,740 --> 00:30:59,820
But actually, the idea of contrastive representation

644
00:30:59,820 --> 00:31:02,380
learning and specifically self-supervised contrastive

645
00:31:02,380 --> 00:31:04,900
representation learning thinks about how

646
00:31:04,900 --> 00:31:07,460
we might take these ideas from metric learning

647
00:31:07,460 --> 00:31:11,620
and also self-supervision to build representation spaces that

648
00:31:11,620 --> 00:31:13,840
are not explicitly supervised.

649
00:31:13,840 --> 00:31:17,220
How do we get a good starting point or good representation

650
00:31:17,220 --> 00:31:20,870
if we don't necessarily have labels?

651
00:31:20,870 --> 00:31:22,950
And why might that, in some ways,

652
00:31:22,950 --> 00:31:27,390
be better than actually training something with explicit labels?

653
00:31:27,390 --> 00:31:30,990
So a common setup for this type of

654
00:31:30,990 --> 00:31:33,175
self-supervised representation learning

655
00:31:33,175 --> 00:31:34,550
would be that we're going to have

656
00:31:34,550 --> 00:31:38,390
some encoder, some function that will map

657
00:31:38,390 --> 00:31:39,810
our data onto a hypersphere.

658
00:31:39,810 --> 00:31:41,852
So again, we're normalizing to enforce everything

659
00:31:41,852 --> 00:31:43,270
to be on the hypersphere.

660
00:31:43,270 --> 00:31:46,870
And we'll define some crossentropy for a softmax

661
00:31:46,870 --> 00:31:49,510
classifier that will discriminate classes

662
00:31:49,510 --> 00:31:52,230
based on similarity.

663
00:31:52,230 --> 00:31:54,950
So if we know that these two images at the bottom

664
00:31:54,950 --> 00:31:57,430
are the same class, and this one is different,

665
00:31:57,430 --> 00:32:00,590
then we're going to try to explicitly pull

666
00:32:00,590 --> 00:32:04,310
positive pairs together and push negative pairs apart.

667
00:32:04,310 --> 00:32:08,350
So essentially, this is something

668
00:32:08,350 --> 00:32:11,210
like a crossentropy loss.

669
00:32:11,210 --> 00:32:16,250
We have an activation of a logarithm of something.

670
00:32:16,250 --> 00:32:20,640
And this kind of looks to us maybe

671
00:32:20,640 --> 00:32:25,440
close to a softmax, something close to a softmax.

672
00:32:25,440 --> 00:32:29,600
So we can use inner products to actually do

673
00:32:29,600 --> 00:32:31,468
this comparison because we've constrained it

674
00:32:31,468 --> 00:32:32,260
to the hypersphere.

675
00:32:32,260 --> 00:32:35,800
So we can look at the angle instead of the distance.

676
00:32:35,800 --> 00:32:40,100
So now we're maximizing the inner product,

677
00:32:40,100 --> 00:32:43,560
which is explicitly trying--

678
00:32:43,560 --> 00:32:45,540
sorry, what am I saying?

679
00:32:45,540 --> 00:32:49,240
No, no, no, so we want we want to make sure

680
00:32:49,240 --> 00:32:52,660
that the inner product is as close as possible to 1

681
00:32:52,660 --> 00:32:55,240
because that would mean that they were as close as

682
00:32:55,240 --> 00:32:57,060
possible on this sphere.

683
00:33:00,440 --> 00:33:02,340
And now instead of minimizing distance,

684
00:33:02,340 --> 00:33:04,520
we can maximize that inner product

685
00:33:04,520 --> 00:33:07,640
that minimizes the angle between the two of them.

686
00:33:07,640 --> 00:33:10,480
And then, essentially, what we want to do

687
00:33:10,480 --> 00:33:13,300
is we want to make this green part as big as possible.

688
00:33:13,300 --> 00:33:16,068
So that's maximizing the inner product

689
00:33:16,068 --> 00:33:17,110
between those two things.

690
00:33:17,110 --> 00:33:21,050
And we want to make the red part as small as possible, which

691
00:33:21,050 --> 00:33:23,430
is going to give us the largest possible overall thing.

692
00:33:26,090 --> 00:33:31,810
Now we assume, for example, that we have a couple

693
00:33:31,810 --> 00:33:36,010
dimensions of symmetry, right.

694
00:33:36,010 --> 00:33:41,930
You can say that we want to have symmetry where we would have

695
00:33:41,930 --> 00:33:46,370
essentially if the first one is your anchor

696
00:33:46,370 --> 00:33:48,350
and the positive example is the second one,

697
00:33:48,350 --> 00:33:50,450
we want the distance between those two be the same

698
00:33:50,450 --> 00:33:52,070
as if you flipped the two.

699
00:33:52,070 --> 00:33:54,182
So it's enforcing some symmetry between them.

700
00:33:54,182 --> 00:33:55,890
The distance should basically be the same

701
00:33:55,890 --> 00:33:58,557
no matter whether your first one is the anchor or the second one

702
00:33:58,557 --> 00:33:59,410
is the anchor.

703
00:33:59,410 --> 00:34:01,310
And we also want to match the marginal.

704
00:34:01,310 --> 00:34:04,170
And kind of what we mean here is for the distribution

705
00:34:04,170 --> 00:34:06,170
over all those positive pairs, we

706
00:34:06,170 --> 00:34:09,449
assume if we marginalize over one of those partners,

707
00:34:09,449 --> 00:34:11,469
we would end up with the data distribution.

708
00:34:11,469 --> 00:34:15,190
So it's starting point distribution over the data.

709
00:34:15,190 --> 00:34:22,230
And so now a really common-- so yeah, this noise contrastive

710
00:34:22,230 --> 00:34:24,670
estimation InfoNCE loss, there's a lot

711
00:34:24,670 --> 00:34:26,870
of similar losses in metric learning

712
00:34:26,870 --> 00:34:30,150
that introduce these types of ideas.

713
00:34:30,150 --> 00:34:34,750
And essentially, if we want to try

714
00:34:34,750 --> 00:34:37,690
to do this specifically self-supervised,

715
00:34:37,690 --> 00:34:40,830
so we don't necessarily know already

716
00:34:40,830 --> 00:34:42,810
that these two things are the same category.

717
00:34:42,810 --> 00:34:45,070
And instead, we're going to do some fancy stuff

718
00:34:45,070 --> 00:34:49,350
to try to force our model to learn that an input data

719
00:34:49,350 --> 00:34:51,938
point is the same as itself after running it

720
00:34:51,938 --> 00:34:53,730
through a bunch of different augmentations,

721
00:34:53,730 --> 00:34:57,150
so perturbing it in a bunch of different ways.

722
00:34:57,150 --> 00:35:02,050
This actually can outperform some supervised pretraining,

723
00:35:02,050 --> 00:35:03,070
which is interesting.

724
00:35:03,070 --> 00:35:07,670
So intuitively, for me, it's like,

725
00:35:07,670 --> 00:35:12,950
why would we want to not have supervision?

726
00:35:12,950 --> 00:35:16,440
Well, one thing is if our supervision is not

727
00:35:16,440 --> 00:35:18,320
well-aligned with the actual task

728
00:35:18,320 --> 00:35:20,860
that we want to use the representation for,

729
00:35:20,860 --> 00:35:23,160
we might learn dimensions of invariance or dimensions

730
00:35:23,160 --> 00:35:25,960
of variance that are explicitly unhelpful.

731
00:35:25,960 --> 00:35:28,320
But if we just do self-supervision,

732
00:35:28,320 --> 00:35:30,320
so we're just trying to learn something

733
00:35:30,320 --> 00:35:33,840
without any explicit definition of what

734
00:35:33,840 --> 00:35:36,440
is similar or dissimilar based on category,

735
00:35:36,440 --> 00:35:39,880
that might actually be better for a bunch

736
00:35:39,880 --> 00:35:42,080
of different possible downstream tasks,

737
00:35:42,080 --> 00:35:44,160
but not necessarily better for everything.

738
00:35:44,160 --> 00:35:46,560
It's a little bit nuanced.

739
00:35:46,560 --> 00:35:48,840
And again, just talking about why we might

740
00:35:48,840 --> 00:35:50,960
map to a hypersphere--

741
00:35:50,960 --> 00:35:54,360
so here, one thing is more stable during training.

742
00:35:54,360 --> 00:35:57,997
Why do we not want to have scale be a dimension of variance?

743
00:35:57,997 --> 00:35:59,580
It's kind of like logistic regression.

744
00:35:59,580 --> 00:36:01,280
It helps you regularize.

745
00:36:01,280 --> 00:36:04,920
And also we get this really nice behavior.

746
00:36:04,920 --> 00:36:08,710
So if you enforce that everything is on a hypersphere.

747
00:36:08,710 --> 00:36:10,960
And you enforce that similar things are close together

748
00:36:10,960 --> 00:36:13,293
on a hypersphere and different things are very far apart

749
00:36:13,293 --> 00:36:14,530
on a hypersphere.

750
00:36:14,530 --> 00:36:18,810
Then you can always build a linear classifier, just a slice

751
00:36:18,810 --> 00:36:21,170
through that hypersphere, that will actually

752
00:36:21,170 --> 00:36:23,690
be linearly separable.

753
00:36:23,690 --> 00:36:25,798
So there's this nice kind of thing where,

754
00:36:25,798 --> 00:36:27,590
no matter what, we can always enforce that.

755
00:36:27,590 --> 00:36:29,090
We build a linear classifier on top

756
00:36:29,090 --> 00:36:31,938
of that hypersphere representation.

757
00:36:31,938 --> 00:36:33,730
How do we actually make it self-supervised?

758
00:36:33,730 --> 00:36:37,650
How do you get this signal if you don't actually

759
00:36:37,650 --> 00:36:40,970
have labels about what's similar and different?

760
00:36:40,970 --> 00:36:45,770
So basically, what people do is they

761
00:36:45,770 --> 00:36:48,610
take all the random stuff, randomly uniform drawn

762
00:36:48,610 --> 00:36:51,850
from the data as negative examples.

763
00:36:51,850 --> 00:36:55,210
And as positive examples, you take your image,

764
00:36:55,210 --> 00:36:58,770
and you do a bunch of weird stuff to it.

765
00:36:58,770 --> 00:37:01,110
So here, we have this original image of a dog,

766
00:37:01,110 --> 00:37:06,070
and we can do crop and resize, crop, resize, and flip.

767
00:37:06,070 --> 00:37:07,610
You can distort the color.

768
00:37:07,610 --> 00:37:09,950
You can drop or jitter the color.

769
00:37:09,950 --> 00:37:10,870
You can rotate it.

770
00:37:10,870 --> 00:37:12,800
You can cut chunks out of it.

771
00:37:12,800 --> 00:37:14,080
You can add Gaussian noise.

772
00:37:14,080 --> 00:37:15,317
You can add Gaussian blur.

773
00:37:15,317 --> 00:37:16,900
You can do things like Sobel filtering

774
00:37:16,900 --> 00:37:21,900
and get you this rough edge kind of representation.

775
00:37:21,900 --> 00:37:24,240
And so then something like SimCLR,

776
00:37:24,240 --> 00:37:26,860
which is one of the original methods that explicitly tried

777
00:37:26,860 --> 00:37:29,620
to teach this type of self-supervised contrastive

778
00:37:29,620 --> 00:37:31,220
learning--

779
00:37:31,220 --> 00:37:33,300
here, for every data point in the batch,

780
00:37:33,300 --> 00:37:35,740
you would generate two different random augmentations

781
00:37:35,740 --> 00:37:36,640
of that data point.

782
00:37:36,640 --> 00:37:39,162
And those two random augmentations are the positives.

783
00:37:39,162 --> 00:37:40,620
And essentially, the intuition here

784
00:37:40,620 --> 00:37:44,220
is you want the exact same thing under some reasonable

785
00:37:44,220 --> 00:37:46,900
perturbations to be mapped very close together,

786
00:37:46,900 --> 00:37:53,140
and then some random set of all of the other things in the batch

787
00:37:53,140 --> 00:37:56,500
are used as negatives for that pair.

788
00:37:56,500 --> 00:37:59,860
So can anyone think of any limitations of this?

789
00:37:59,860 --> 00:38:00,618
Yeah?

790
00:38:00,618 --> 00:38:02,660
AUDIENCE: If you happen to have multiple pictures

791
00:38:02,660 --> 00:38:04,598
of the same thing in the data set.

792
00:38:04,598 --> 00:38:06,140
SARA BEERY: Yeah, so what if you have

793
00:38:06,140 --> 00:38:09,460
multiple pictures of the same thing in the data set?

794
00:38:09,460 --> 00:38:12,710
So it turns out, and we'll get into this, that that actually

795
00:38:12,710 --> 00:38:13,770
is a challenge.

796
00:38:13,770 --> 00:38:16,470
And the assumption is that, well, maybe there's

797
00:38:16,470 --> 00:38:19,150
something in terms of similarity that we would still

798
00:38:19,150 --> 00:38:21,550
possibly want to capture if you have two dogs that

799
00:38:21,550 --> 00:38:23,670
are exactly the same under perturbation

800
00:38:23,670 --> 00:38:27,710
versus another dog, for example, or another lab or even

801
00:38:27,710 --> 00:38:29,910
another picture of that exact same dog.

802
00:38:29,910 --> 00:38:36,490
But definitely these types of positive negatives,

803
00:38:36,490 --> 00:38:39,818
almost in a way, are confusing when you're actually

804
00:38:39,818 --> 00:38:41,110
learning these representations.

805
00:38:41,110 --> 00:38:42,830
And there's explicitly work that shows

806
00:38:42,830 --> 00:38:46,670
that if you can remove those, even by using something

807
00:38:46,670 --> 00:38:49,070
like explicit supervision, it can actually really improve

808
00:38:49,070 --> 00:38:50,590
a representation.

809
00:38:50,590 --> 00:38:51,270
Yeah?

810
00:38:51,270 --> 00:38:53,630
AUDIENCE: Is there an analogy to the perturbations

811
00:38:53,630 --> 00:39:00,910
for nonimage data types, for example, that are commonly used?

812
00:39:00,910 --> 00:39:02,815
SARA BEERY: Yeah, so for example here,

813
00:39:02,815 --> 00:39:04,190
we're talking about SimCLR, which

814
00:39:04,190 --> 00:39:08,440
is explicitly an image-focused contrastive thing.

815
00:39:08,440 --> 00:39:14,260
So those perturbations are going to be data-specific.

816
00:39:14,260 --> 00:39:19,240
So for example, reasonable perturbations on images

817
00:39:19,240 --> 00:39:22,360
are things that we actually have some intuition that we

818
00:39:22,360 --> 00:39:23,700
want to be invariant to.

819
00:39:23,700 --> 00:39:26,160
We want to recognize the object is the same

820
00:39:26,160 --> 00:39:28,160
and is invariant under flips, for example.

821
00:39:28,160 --> 00:39:31,040
This is a strong assumption that we're building in.

822
00:39:31,040 --> 00:39:32,478
And there are actually some cases

823
00:39:32,478 --> 00:39:35,020
where we explicitly don't want to build in those assumptions.

824
00:39:35,020 --> 00:39:36,560
If your downstream representation

825
00:39:36,560 --> 00:39:38,268
needs to be able to detect the difference

826
00:39:38,268 --> 00:39:40,280
between right and left shoes, if you

827
00:39:40,280 --> 00:39:43,100
use random flipping as an augmentation,

828
00:39:43,100 --> 00:39:45,698
it makes that almost impossible because if you

829
00:39:45,698 --> 00:39:47,740
say this is a right shoe and this is a right shoe

830
00:39:47,740 --> 00:39:49,740
and this is a left shoe and this is a left shoe,

831
00:39:49,740 --> 00:39:52,000
your model is incredibly confused.

832
00:39:52,000 --> 00:39:56,040
So the same would be possible for any other type of data.

833
00:39:56,040 --> 00:40:02,000
Say you had, for example, data of specific molecules.

834
00:40:02,000 --> 00:40:04,760
You then would have to inject some domain knowledge in terms

835
00:40:04,760 --> 00:40:08,370
of what types of augmentations of those molecules

836
00:40:08,370 --> 00:40:10,810
are reasonable, where we would kind of want to maintain

837
00:40:10,810 --> 00:40:12,690
similarity, and what types of augmentations

838
00:40:12,690 --> 00:40:15,370
are actually potentially unreasonable.

839
00:40:15,370 --> 00:40:16,730
Same for text.

840
00:40:16,730 --> 00:40:18,370
Same for video.

841
00:40:18,370 --> 00:40:20,050
So the choice of augmentations actually

842
00:40:20,050 --> 00:40:22,653
does become quite important.

843
00:40:22,653 --> 00:40:24,570
You can think of a lot of different variations

844
00:40:24,570 --> 00:40:26,970
of this type of contrastive learning

845
00:40:26,970 --> 00:40:29,530
that use self-supervision-- yeah?

846
00:40:29,530 --> 00:40:32,350
AUDIENCE: On the previous slide, I just have a question.

847
00:40:32,350 --> 00:40:35,190
Why doesn't the model just learn something really stupid,

848
00:40:35,190 --> 00:40:37,910
like what about this setup?

849
00:40:37,910 --> 00:40:39,630
Is it going to guarantee, for instance,

850
00:40:39,630 --> 00:40:42,250
that two golden retrievers are going

851
00:40:42,250 --> 00:40:45,410
to-- say you get a different image of a golden retriever,

852
00:40:45,410 --> 00:40:47,530
and you make two augmentations of it,

853
00:40:47,530 --> 00:40:51,210
and then you pick a negative sample that's now a horse.

854
00:40:51,210 --> 00:40:53,070
There's nothing about this, I think,

855
00:40:53,070 --> 00:40:55,237
that says that those two golden retrievers are going

856
00:40:55,237 --> 00:40:57,250
to end up close to this picture golden retriever

857
00:40:57,250 --> 00:40:58,910
in your representation space.

858
00:41:01,930 --> 00:41:03,910
SARA BEERY: Right, so if you augment this one,

859
00:41:03,910 --> 00:41:06,680
you augment that one, that one, and you force it close together,

860
00:41:06,680 --> 00:41:10,035
there's nothing from this particular training step that

861
00:41:10,035 --> 00:41:11,660
would say that either of them should be

862
00:41:11,660 --> 00:41:13,940
closer to the original image.

863
00:41:13,940 --> 00:41:16,820
But now you're going to sample these over and over

864
00:41:16,820 --> 00:41:19,060
during training in many different combinations

865
00:41:19,060 --> 00:41:21,420
and with many different randomizations.

866
00:41:21,420 --> 00:41:24,500
And so the assumption is, essentially,

867
00:41:24,500 --> 00:41:27,100
though there might be some things in there that

868
00:41:27,100 --> 00:41:30,340
are more or less confusing, that generally, over time,

869
00:41:30,340 --> 00:41:33,540
you're going to learn that golden retriever-like things

870
00:41:33,540 --> 00:41:35,240
should all be somewhat close together

871
00:41:35,240 --> 00:41:36,740
because, more often than not, you're

872
00:41:36,740 --> 00:41:39,340
going to get two augmentations of a golden retriever

873
00:41:39,340 --> 00:41:41,020
and a boat, not two augmentations

874
00:41:41,020 --> 00:41:44,340
of a golden retriever and another golden retriever.

875
00:41:44,340 --> 00:41:45,980
Does that make sense?

876
00:41:45,980 --> 00:41:51,180
So I think it comes out in the noise kind of over time

877
00:41:51,180 --> 00:41:54,460
and, over many, many different types of categories,

878
00:41:54,460 --> 00:41:57,340
does equalize or get to something

879
00:41:57,340 --> 00:42:00,740
that has learned the signal you want it to learn.

880
00:42:00,740 --> 00:42:02,660
So there are a lot of different clever ways

881
00:42:02,660 --> 00:42:06,630
to think about how to get that similarity signal that

882
00:42:06,630 --> 00:42:11,070
isn't necessarily just massively augmenting.

883
00:42:11,070 --> 00:42:12,790
So one example is if you actually

884
00:42:12,790 --> 00:42:17,830
have data of something like a scene

885
00:42:17,830 --> 00:42:21,030
and the depth image for that scene,

886
00:42:21,030 --> 00:42:24,830
then you can, for example, try to learn representations

887
00:42:24,830 --> 00:42:29,170
that put things close together if it's actually--

888
00:42:29,170 --> 00:42:32,790
the image of the scene closely matches the depth of the scene.

889
00:42:32,790 --> 00:42:35,910
So you can learn representations that

890
00:42:35,910 --> 00:42:38,110
capture something about scene structure

891
00:42:38,110 --> 00:42:43,190
by explicitly leveraging RGB-D during training.

892
00:42:43,190 --> 00:42:46,470
Or you could think about representation learning

893
00:42:46,470 --> 00:42:49,670
over video, where you take frames randomly sampled

894
00:42:49,670 --> 00:42:52,470
from the same video as positives and a frame

895
00:42:52,470 --> 00:42:53,883
from another video as negatives.

896
00:42:53,883 --> 00:42:55,550
Now, your model needs to learn something

897
00:42:55,550 --> 00:42:59,430
about possibly potential similarity over time.

898
00:42:59,430 --> 00:43:03,610
And there's lots of different works that do of thing.

899
00:43:03,610 --> 00:43:08,890
You can think about using vision language as views

900
00:43:08,890 --> 00:43:10,450
in some way of the same scene.

901
00:43:10,450 --> 00:43:13,545
You have the text description of an image and the image itself.

902
00:43:13,545 --> 00:43:15,170
You try to push those together, and you

903
00:43:15,170 --> 00:43:17,570
try to push them far away from an image that doesn't

904
00:43:17,570 --> 00:43:19,113
match that text description.

905
00:43:19,113 --> 00:43:20,530
So there's a lot of ways of trying

906
00:43:20,530 --> 00:43:23,430
to think about how you can get this self-supervised signal.

907
00:43:23,430 --> 00:43:25,490
And a lot of modern work actually

908
00:43:25,490 --> 00:43:27,970
really is just people coming up with creative ways

909
00:43:27,970 --> 00:43:30,870
to supervise models without requiring labels

910
00:43:30,870 --> 00:43:32,330
because labels are--

911
00:43:32,330 --> 00:43:36,490
handcrafted, human-generated labels are very expensive.

912
00:43:36,490 --> 00:43:43,210
So essentially, what is this method doing?

913
00:43:43,210 --> 00:43:47,770
There's kind of two ingredients here to making this work.

914
00:43:47,770 --> 00:43:51,250
One is the contrastive loss, so which specific form

915
00:43:51,250 --> 00:43:53,530
of contrastive loss you use.

916
00:43:53,530 --> 00:43:55,570
And the other one is the data.

917
00:43:55,570 --> 00:43:58,390
And by the data, I don't just mean the underlying data set.

918
00:43:58,390 --> 00:44:00,410
I also mean specifically which types

919
00:44:00,410 --> 00:44:02,140
of positive and negative pairs we

920
00:44:02,140 --> 00:44:04,820
surface to the model over time.

921
00:44:04,820 --> 00:44:07,620
So if we dig into the contrastive loss,

922
00:44:07,620 --> 00:44:10,380
and we think of this, again, as something like a cross entropy

923
00:44:10,380 --> 00:44:14,940
loss that tries to distinguish between data points,

924
00:44:14,940 --> 00:44:17,620
another way that you can motivate this loss

925
00:44:17,620 --> 00:44:20,180
mathematically is you can try to frame it

926
00:44:20,180 --> 00:44:23,940
as maximizing the mutual information

927
00:44:23,940 --> 00:44:28,620
between positive examples in the pairs.

928
00:44:28,620 --> 00:44:32,060
And so essentially, if you think of it that way,

929
00:44:32,060 --> 00:44:35,980
you're really just trying to maximize a lower

930
00:44:35,980 --> 00:44:40,980
bound on mutual information between views of the same thing.

931
00:44:40,980 --> 00:44:44,980
And there's been some interesting work, specifically

932
00:44:44,980 --> 00:44:50,300
this work that showed that, actually, if, instead of using

933
00:44:50,300 --> 00:44:55,180
the standard contrastive losses, you just explicitly maximize

934
00:44:55,180 --> 00:44:58,060
mutual information alone, it actually worsened

935
00:44:58,060 --> 00:45:00,060
downstream performance.

936
00:45:00,060 --> 00:45:02,710
And looser bounds with simpler critics

937
00:45:02,710 --> 00:45:04,090
led to better representation.

938
00:45:04,090 --> 00:45:06,350
So essentially, the contrastive loss

939
00:45:06,350 --> 00:45:07,770
has to be doing something else.

940
00:45:07,770 --> 00:45:10,910
It's not just maximizing the mutual information

941
00:45:10,910 --> 00:45:13,150
between things that are similar.

942
00:45:13,150 --> 00:45:15,110
So what else is it doing?

943
00:45:15,110 --> 00:45:17,350
So we'd said that we had a couple of properties

944
00:45:17,350 --> 00:45:20,010
that we liked that made representations good.

945
00:45:20,010 --> 00:45:22,650
We had concentration alignment.

946
00:45:22,650 --> 00:45:24,790
So data from the same class is close.

947
00:45:24,790 --> 00:45:27,390
We had separation, which means data from different classes

948
00:45:27,390 --> 00:45:28,510
are far apart.

949
00:45:28,510 --> 00:45:30,390
We don't lose information.

950
00:45:30,390 --> 00:45:33,050
And then we had robustness to irrelevant perturbations.

951
00:45:33,050 --> 00:45:35,430
We don't want to change something in some way

952
00:45:35,430 --> 00:45:39,070
that we think is irrelevant and have it change or move really

953
00:45:39,070 --> 00:45:40,330
far in our space.

954
00:45:43,030 --> 00:45:48,623
So you can see how, explicitly, the alignment is really

955
00:45:48,623 --> 00:45:50,290
built into that loss from the beginning.

956
00:45:50,290 --> 00:45:52,920
We're saying we want to pull similar samples together.

957
00:45:52,920 --> 00:45:54,170
So that's enforcing alignment.

958
00:45:54,170 --> 00:45:56,910
Similar samples should have similar features.

959
00:45:56,910 --> 00:46:03,920
And then you can talk about this uniformity, which is essentially

960
00:46:03,920 --> 00:46:06,007
saying we want to push to similar samples apart,

961
00:46:06,007 --> 00:46:07,840
and we're pushing all the dissimilar samples

962
00:46:07,840 --> 00:46:08,860
apart kind of equally.

963
00:46:08,860 --> 00:46:10,360
You can imagine that the optimal way

964
00:46:10,360 --> 00:46:14,880
to do that would be to cover the entire hypersphere because if we

965
00:46:14,880 --> 00:46:16,460
don't use part of the hypersphere,

966
00:46:16,460 --> 00:46:17,960
then there's actually a way we could

967
00:46:17,960 --> 00:46:22,040
have pushed even further apart things that are different.

968
00:46:22,040 --> 00:46:25,280
So here, this actually pushes apart all of the classes.

969
00:46:25,280 --> 00:46:25,900
We're here.

970
00:46:25,900 --> 00:46:28,600
Every class is, again, one point because we've

971
00:46:28,600 --> 00:46:31,160
said that the only information we have

972
00:46:31,160 --> 00:46:35,000
is that this image is the same as itself under perturbations.

973
00:46:35,000 --> 00:46:38,000
So we're really pushing apart all the data points

974
00:46:38,000 --> 00:46:40,000
on that hypersphere, which is kind

975
00:46:40,000 --> 00:46:42,880
of pushing for that uniformity in our feature space.

976
00:46:42,880 --> 00:46:48,000
And that could be as seen as a way to enforce separation.

977
00:46:48,000 --> 00:46:51,960
So you can actually see this, which is really interesting.

978
00:46:51,960 --> 00:46:57,480
So if you take CIFAR-10 and you build these encoders that

979
00:46:57,480 --> 00:47:00,210
encode them into the S1 feature space

980
00:47:00,210 --> 00:47:03,190
with the hypersphere on one dimension or two dimensions,

981
00:47:03,190 --> 00:47:05,890
it's just a circle, and you visualize feature distributions

982
00:47:05,890 --> 00:47:08,170
on the validation set, you can see

983
00:47:08,170 --> 00:47:10,550
that if you use unsupervised contrastive learning,

984
00:47:10,550 --> 00:47:13,050
you get this distribution that is

985
00:47:13,050 --> 00:47:16,770
properly uniform around that circle and that embedding space.

986
00:47:16,770 --> 00:47:19,930
Whereas if you actually use supervised predictive learning,

987
00:47:19,930 --> 00:47:23,210
you get a feature distribution that's not uniform.

988
00:47:23,210 --> 00:47:25,430
And if you just randomly initialize the network,

989
00:47:25,430 --> 00:47:27,290
it's far from uniform.

990
00:47:27,290 --> 00:47:30,590
We really don't get anything close to this uniformity.

991
00:47:30,590 --> 00:47:32,950
So you can see that almost it's actually happening.

992
00:47:32,950 --> 00:47:34,090
Something in the way that we're doing

993
00:47:34,090 --> 00:47:35,715
these unsupervised contrastive learning

994
00:47:35,715 --> 00:47:38,930
is really pushing and enforcing uniformity

995
00:47:38,930 --> 00:47:41,330
across the data points.

996
00:47:41,330 --> 00:47:43,530
So how can we quantify this?

997
00:47:43,530 --> 00:47:45,770
So the idea is that you might want to explicitly

998
00:47:45,770 --> 00:47:49,690
be able to study alignment and uniformity, so things being

999
00:47:49,690 --> 00:47:53,390
close together and things being spread apart, using metrics.

1000
00:47:53,390 --> 00:47:57,900
We want, actually, a way to quantify how aligned

1001
00:47:57,900 --> 00:48:00,900
or how uniform a representation is.

1002
00:48:00,900 --> 00:48:04,540
So for uniform, for alignment, we just

1003
00:48:04,540 --> 00:48:07,740
do something like looking at the expectation

1004
00:48:07,740 --> 00:48:12,860
over the distance between features for positive pairs.

1005
00:48:12,860 --> 00:48:13,960
This is pretty intuitive.

1006
00:48:13,960 --> 00:48:17,300
So you're just saying my alignment metric

1007
00:48:17,300 --> 00:48:20,540
is how far apart do I think any two positive examples are

1008
00:48:20,540 --> 00:48:23,260
going to be in expectation.

1009
00:48:23,260 --> 00:48:25,540
And then, for uniformity, instead

1010
00:48:25,540 --> 00:48:27,780
of trying to explicitly say the expected

1011
00:48:27,780 --> 00:48:30,820
difference between negative pairs,

1012
00:48:30,820 --> 00:48:33,820
you can do a transformation of this using the Gaussian

1013
00:48:33,820 --> 00:48:35,060
potential.

1014
00:48:35,060 --> 00:48:38,980
And then you can look at using the logarithm of the expected

1015
00:48:38,980 --> 00:48:41,860
pairwise Gaussian potential, which

1016
00:48:41,860 --> 00:48:45,380
is essentially e to the minus squared distance.

1017
00:48:45,380 --> 00:48:50,500
And this puts more focus on the smallest distances.

1018
00:48:50,500 --> 00:48:53,020
And this objective of goal has a lot

1019
00:48:53,020 --> 00:48:54,400
of connections throughout math.

1020
00:48:54,400 --> 00:48:56,150
And it turns out the solution that

1021
00:48:56,150 --> 00:49:00,750
optimizes this is the one that maximally spreads out

1022
00:49:00,750 --> 00:49:02,233
the points on the hypersphere.

1023
00:49:02,233 --> 00:49:04,150
So the uniform distribution on the hypersphere

1024
00:49:04,150 --> 00:49:07,430
is explicitly the solution to this.

1025
00:49:07,430 --> 00:49:11,750
And so now the uniform distribution on the hypersphere

1026
00:49:11,750 --> 00:49:14,150
becomes the unique measure that minimizes

1027
00:49:14,150 --> 00:49:18,790
expected pairwise potential between negative pairs.

1028
00:49:18,790 --> 00:49:21,930
So now if we want to dig into our explicit contrastive loss,

1029
00:49:21,930 --> 00:49:24,410
one way to do that is we take asymptotics.

1030
00:49:24,410 --> 00:49:30,450
We assume, OK, what happens as you take things to infinity?

1031
00:49:30,450 --> 00:49:32,750
That's a good way to understand behavior generally

1032
00:49:32,750 --> 00:49:36,990
when you're looking at mathematical properties.

1033
00:49:36,990 --> 00:49:41,230
So here, without getting too formal,

1034
00:49:41,230 --> 00:49:43,750
we want to make connections between alignment and uniformity

1035
00:49:43,750 --> 00:49:45,950
and our contrastive loss.

1036
00:49:45,950 --> 00:49:48,310
So since our definitions are expectations,

1037
00:49:48,310 --> 00:49:50,630
we'll look at the asymptotic behavior,

1038
00:49:50,630 --> 00:49:54,800
specifically taking the limit as the number of negative samples

1039
00:49:54,800 --> 00:49:55,960
goes to infinity.

1040
00:49:55,960 --> 00:50:01,360
So say we have infinite negative examples for any given positive.

1041
00:50:01,360 --> 00:50:03,240
Then you can reformulate this to getting

1042
00:50:03,240 --> 00:50:05,840
basically the combination two different terms that

1043
00:50:05,840 --> 00:50:07,480
are expectations.

1044
00:50:07,480 --> 00:50:10,760
And we're putting them here in green and in red

1045
00:50:10,760 --> 00:50:14,760
because, basically, the first component of this

1046
00:50:14,760 --> 00:50:18,880
gets minimized if every single point of the same category

1047
00:50:18,880 --> 00:50:21,120
has the exact same embedding.

1048
00:50:21,120 --> 00:50:25,840
So essentially, if the distance between positive pairs is just 0

1049
00:50:25,840 --> 00:50:28,280
and the second term gets minimized,

1050
00:50:28,280 --> 00:50:30,973
if those collapsed points are-- all the points

1051
00:50:30,973 --> 00:50:32,640
are spread out as far as possible, which

1052
00:50:32,640 --> 00:50:35,280
is that uniform distribution.

1053
00:50:35,280 --> 00:50:38,760
So the first term gets minimized if and only

1054
00:50:38,760 --> 00:50:41,120
if f is perfectly aligned.

1055
00:50:41,120 --> 00:50:44,760
All the things that are same are exactly the same embedding.

1056
00:50:44,760 --> 00:50:49,060
And then the second one is minimized

1057
00:50:49,060 --> 00:50:51,400
if we get perfect uniformity, which is explicitly

1058
00:50:51,400 --> 00:50:52,820
the uniform feature distribution,

1059
00:50:52,820 --> 00:50:55,610
pushing everything as far apart as possible.

1060
00:50:55,610 --> 00:50:59,130
And now these are actually measurable quantities.

1061
00:50:59,130 --> 00:51:03,570
So now you can actually look at representation quality

1062
00:51:03,570 --> 00:51:07,330
as defined by alignment versus uniformity

1063
00:51:07,330 --> 00:51:12,770
and look at how accurate, for example,

1064
00:51:12,770 --> 00:51:15,568
prediction is on a validation set.

1065
00:51:15,568 --> 00:51:18,110
And what you can see is you take a bunch of different models,

1066
00:51:18,110 --> 00:51:19,568
so every single one of these points

1067
00:51:19,568 --> 00:51:21,865
would be a different representation space

1068
00:51:21,865 --> 00:51:23,490
or different thing that's been learned,

1069
00:51:23,490 --> 00:51:26,210
and you can plot them in terms of their alignment

1070
00:51:26,210 --> 00:51:29,410
versus their uniformity as measured by these quantities.

1071
00:51:29,410 --> 00:51:32,450
And you can show that the things that have the highest validation

1072
00:51:32,450 --> 00:51:36,490
accuracy are the best possible minimizers of both alignment

1073
00:51:36,490 --> 00:51:37,770
and uniformity.

1074
00:51:37,770 --> 00:51:40,770
So this kind of red that goes into blue that goes back

1075
00:51:40,770 --> 00:51:44,770
into red explicitly tells us in some nice, measurable way

1076
00:51:44,770 --> 00:51:46,370
that alignment and uniformity are

1077
00:51:46,370 --> 00:51:49,450
in fact good qualities for a representation space,

1078
00:51:49,450 --> 00:51:53,620
and they map towards good accuracy on downstream tasks.

1079
00:51:53,620 --> 00:51:56,980
And you can show this over several different data sets,

1080
00:51:56,980 --> 00:52:00,820
though you will see that simpler data sets show this better

1081
00:52:00,820 --> 00:52:04,340
than more complicated ones.

1082
00:52:04,340 --> 00:52:07,080
So what's the contrastive loss doing?

1083
00:52:07,080 --> 00:52:09,460
So the loss function itself, we've just shown,

1084
00:52:09,460 --> 00:52:14,060
explicitly encourages alignment and uniformity or concentration

1085
00:52:14,060 --> 00:52:15,620
and separation.

1086
00:52:15,620 --> 00:52:18,760
So then what is the selection of positive and negative pairs

1087
00:52:18,760 --> 00:52:20,700
encouraging?

1088
00:52:20,700 --> 00:52:23,500
We talked about how we generate those positive and negative

1089
00:52:23,500 --> 00:52:26,580
pairs is through maybe all these different augmentations.

1090
00:52:26,580 --> 00:52:32,420
So what are we teaching a model via our choice of pairs?

1091
00:52:32,420 --> 00:52:35,220
So augmentations of the same data point should be close.

1092
00:52:37,820 --> 00:52:41,500
And then we want a learned representation

1093
00:52:41,500 --> 00:52:43,380
that's invariant to perturbations

1094
00:52:43,380 --> 00:52:45,000
induced by data augmentations.

1095
00:52:45,000 --> 00:52:47,180
So we're trying to learn invariance

1096
00:52:47,180 --> 00:52:50,440
to the set of augmentations we explicitly put in.

1097
00:52:50,440 --> 00:52:54,320
And it turns out that finding the right invariances

1098
00:52:54,320 --> 00:52:55,880
can be really hard.

1099
00:52:55,880 --> 00:52:57,520
We talked about the molecule thing.

1100
00:52:57,520 --> 00:53:01,440
We talked about in the right and left shoe thing.

1101
00:53:01,440 --> 00:53:06,400
And so there might actually be some explicit invariances

1102
00:53:06,400 --> 00:53:08,440
like, for example, the ones we talked

1103
00:53:08,440 --> 00:53:10,467
about in the geometric deep learning lecture

1104
00:53:10,467 --> 00:53:12,300
that you could actually explicitly hardcode.

1105
00:53:12,300 --> 00:53:14,465
If you want to be symmetrically invariant,

1106
00:53:14,465 --> 00:53:15,840
you could build symmetry directly

1107
00:53:15,840 --> 00:53:18,850
into your representation.

1108
00:53:18,850 --> 00:53:20,600
And maybe there are some cases where you'd

1109
00:53:20,600 --> 00:53:23,320
want to use one or the other.

1110
00:53:23,320 --> 00:53:26,622
And it turns out that there are a lot of types of invariances

1111
00:53:26,622 --> 00:53:28,080
that we might want to have that can

1112
00:53:28,080 --> 00:53:30,320
be really difficult to define mathematically.

1113
00:53:30,320 --> 00:53:33,400
And so this kind of experimental or maybe slightly more

1114
00:53:33,400 --> 00:53:35,860
brute force approach of just explicitly saying, hey,

1115
00:53:35,860 --> 00:53:39,720
if I do this to this thing, I want it to be the same,

1116
00:53:39,720 --> 00:53:42,160
you can build those invariances in without actually

1117
00:53:42,160 --> 00:53:45,040
being able to mathematically define explicitly

1118
00:53:45,040 --> 00:53:47,060
what it means to be, for example,

1119
00:53:47,060 --> 00:53:50,650
invariant to pose within a scene or distance

1120
00:53:50,650 --> 00:53:53,250
in these complex geometric spaces,

1121
00:53:53,250 --> 00:53:56,810
particularly for nonrigid objects.

1122
00:53:56,810 --> 00:53:59,370
So what we're saying here is essentially

1123
00:53:59,370 --> 00:54:02,050
that the data is explicitly the dimension that

1124
00:54:02,050 --> 00:54:05,490
encourages the robustness, that third category that we really

1125
00:54:05,490 --> 00:54:07,610
wanted in our representation.

1126
00:54:07,610 --> 00:54:12,690
That robustness to perturbations is coming just from our data.

1127
00:54:12,690 --> 00:54:16,090
So this means that to make all of this work,

1128
00:54:16,090 --> 00:54:19,930
you specifically want to have this set of things that

1129
00:54:19,930 --> 00:54:25,050
help us get those losses to converge and encourage

1130
00:54:25,050 --> 00:54:27,410
the right types of robustness.

1131
00:54:27,410 --> 00:54:32,490
And it turns out, for example, that these four things,

1132
00:54:32,490 --> 00:54:36,210
really heavy data augmentation, like not just simple stuff,

1133
00:54:36,210 --> 00:54:40,010
doing really, really weird things to your inputs within

1134
00:54:40,010 --> 00:54:42,750
reason, having these projection heads--

1135
00:54:42,750 --> 00:54:44,930
we'll talk about this in a second--

1136
00:54:44,930 --> 00:54:50,380
having very large batch sizes, and selecting the correct data

1137
00:54:50,380 --> 00:54:52,158
pairs and hard negative examples,

1138
00:54:52,158 --> 00:54:53,700
these are really the ingredients that

1139
00:54:53,700 --> 00:54:55,740
make this self-supervised contrastive learning

1140
00:54:55,740 --> 00:54:58,180
work much better.

1141
00:54:58,180 --> 00:55:01,100
And you can actually see this in the literature.

1142
00:55:01,100 --> 00:55:05,580
So here, they're explicitly ablating in the SimCLR paper

1143
00:55:05,580 --> 00:55:08,020
how these different transformations

1144
00:55:08,020 --> 00:55:09,620
affect the performance.

1145
00:55:09,620 --> 00:55:11,580
And it turns out that the performance

1146
00:55:11,580 --> 00:55:14,060
of your downstream classifier, if you

1147
00:55:14,060 --> 00:55:16,580
do self-supervised representation learning

1148
00:55:16,580 --> 00:55:21,160
using only crops, for example, is significantly lower,

1149
00:55:21,160 --> 00:55:24,740
like 15% almost lower, than if you

1150
00:55:24,740 --> 00:55:29,060
use all of the entire set of augmentations shown here

1151
00:55:29,060 --> 00:55:30,620
on the left.

1152
00:55:30,620 --> 00:55:32,660
So the data augmentation definitely

1153
00:55:32,660 --> 00:55:35,580
plays a huge role here.

1154
00:55:35,580 --> 00:55:37,940
Another thing that actually plays a huge role

1155
00:55:37,940 --> 00:55:40,540
is this idea of a projection head.

1156
00:55:40,540 --> 00:55:44,140
So here, what people found experimentally

1157
00:55:44,140 --> 00:55:48,510
is that if, instead of applying your contrastive loss directly

1158
00:55:48,510 --> 00:55:52,010
to the feature representation you're trying to learn,

1159
00:55:52,010 --> 00:55:55,950
you enable some learned projection g that's

1160
00:55:55,950 --> 00:55:59,230
quite simple from h, which is the representation space you're

1161
00:55:59,230 --> 00:56:03,470
planning to use, and some z where you're actually

1162
00:56:03,470 --> 00:56:07,330
going to maximize the agreement between similar pairs,

1163
00:56:07,330 --> 00:56:09,430
you're actually applying that contrastive loss,

1164
00:56:09,430 --> 00:56:15,448
that this ends up also really helping performance downstream.

1165
00:56:15,448 --> 00:56:16,990
And g will be something quite simple,

1166
00:56:16,990 --> 00:56:20,710
a linear projection or a very small multilayer perceptron.

1167
00:56:20,710 --> 00:56:22,990
And then so you don't actually use z,

1168
00:56:22,990 --> 00:56:24,670
which is like this projected space where

1169
00:56:24,670 --> 00:56:27,330
you're calculating your loss as your representation.

1170
00:56:27,330 --> 00:56:29,550
Instead, you use h.

1171
00:56:29,550 --> 00:56:32,137
And this projection had improved performance.

1172
00:56:32,137 --> 00:56:34,470
And I think one of the ways that people have intuitively

1173
00:56:34,470 --> 00:56:40,190
tried to explain it is that instead of--

1174
00:56:40,190 --> 00:56:43,870
it lets the model learn a representation that keeps

1175
00:56:43,870 --> 00:56:46,080
a little bit more variance.

1176
00:56:46,080 --> 00:56:48,400
So for example, if you're trying to explicitly enforce

1177
00:56:48,400 --> 00:56:50,640
this contrastive loss, you're really

1178
00:56:50,640 --> 00:56:56,080
trying to push things completely together that are augmentations

1179
00:56:56,080 --> 00:56:57,480
of the same object.

1180
00:56:57,480 --> 00:57:00,040
But actually, maintaining some dimensions

1181
00:57:00,040 --> 00:57:03,320
of variance to some of those differences, it seems,

1182
00:57:03,320 --> 00:57:05,680
may be a helpful thing.

1183
00:57:05,680 --> 00:57:08,180
This isn't something that's been super formalized,

1184
00:57:08,180 --> 00:57:09,880
but it's some high-level intuition

1185
00:57:09,880 --> 00:57:13,200
for why we might want to use this representation as opposed

1186
00:57:13,200 --> 00:57:15,680
to the thing we're actually calculating loss over.

1187
00:57:15,680 --> 00:57:20,160
And actually, I see some synonyms to this in transformers

1188
00:57:20,160 --> 00:57:22,820
because when you think about a transformer,

1189
00:57:22,820 --> 00:57:27,240
there's one set of projections where you're calculating

1190
00:57:27,240 --> 00:57:29,443
similarity between things.

1191
00:57:29,443 --> 00:57:31,360
And then there's this other set of projections

1192
00:57:31,360 --> 00:57:33,840
that you use to actually pass information.

1193
00:57:33,840 --> 00:57:36,880
And so it kind of, again, seems to suggest

1194
00:57:36,880 --> 00:57:39,935
that it might be valuable to have differences

1195
00:57:39,935 --> 00:57:41,560
between the representations we actually

1196
00:57:41,560 --> 00:57:44,130
use to keep information and the representations

1197
00:57:44,130 --> 00:57:48,290
we use to calculate similarity or difference.

1198
00:57:48,290 --> 00:57:51,530
So if you actually look at this, you

1199
00:57:51,530 --> 00:57:56,690
can see you have these gains between no projection

1200
00:57:56,690 --> 00:58:01,690
at all versus different dimensionalities

1201
00:58:01,690 --> 00:58:05,850
of those projections and linear versus nonlinear projections.

1202
00:58:05,850 --> 00:58:10,290
And again, h maybe gets to keep some invariance

1203
00:58:10,290 --> 00:58:13,690
to dimensions of augmentation.

1204
00:58:13,690 --> 00:58:16,570
Batch size has also been shown to be really important.

1205
00:58:16,570 --> 00:58:20,710
So SimCLR uses all the points in a batch as negative examples.

1206
00:58:20,710 --> 00:58:24,010
And it turns out that the number of negative examples you have

1207
00:58:24,010 --> 00:58:27,690
at every given point really significantly improves

1208
00:58:27,690 --> 00:58:30,130
performance, particularly when you have--

1209
00:58:30,130 --> 00:58:32,050
early on in training, you can see

1210
00:58:32,050 --> 00:58:35,810
that the difference is close to 10% between having

1211
00:58:35,810 --> 00:58:41,050
a batch size of 256 versus a batch size of 8192.

1212
00:58:41,050 --> 00:58:44,440
This is another reason why all of us are building GPUs-- well,

1213
00:58:44,440 --> 00:58:46,180
I say all of us--

1214
00:58:46,180 --> 00:58:48,540
NVIDIA is building GPUs that have

1215
00:58:48,540 --> 00:58:50,100
bigger and bigger and bigger memory

1216
00:58:50,100 --> 00:58:52,980
because we need to be able to capture batch sizes that

1217
00:58:52,980 --> 00:58:56,700
are really big to be able to maximize performance.

1218
00:58:56,700 --> 00:58:58,780
And it turns out this is very expensive.

1219
00:58:58,780 --> 00:59:01,260
Needing to have huge batch sizes is

1220
00:59:01,260 --> 00:59:04,260
costly from a computational perspective.

1221
00:59:04,260 --> 00:59:07,040
And there's a lot of efforts to try to make that more efficient.

1222
00:59:07,040 --> 00:59:09,600
So Moco, for example, you can go read.

1223
00:59:09,600 --> 00:59:11,620
It was an improvement upon SimCLR

1224
00:59:11,620 --> 00:59:15,220
that explicitly tried to make that the batch size dimension

1225
00:59:15,220 --> 00:59:18,140
a little bit more efficient.

1226
00:59:18,140 --> 00:59:21,340
And then, finally, we talked about the selection

1227
00:59:21,340 --> 00:59:23,540
of negative examples, how it's really important

1228
00:59:23,540 --> 00:59:29,080
to try to make sure that negative pairs are informative.

1229
00:59:29,080 --> 00:59:30,800
We want to find the hard cases.

1230
00:59:30,800 --> 00:59:33,900
We're actually getting signal from our loss.

1231
00:59:33,900 --> 00:59:38,660
And it turns out that if you actually have labels--

1232
00:59:38,660 --> 00:59:42,550
and so here, labels would be maybe categories

1233
00:59:42,550 --> 00:59:44,710
from your set of classes that you're

1234
00:59:44,710 --> 00:59:46,910
interested in using your downstream representation

1235
00:59:46,910 --> 00:59:48,030
to categorize--

1236
00:59:48,030 --> 00:59:48,530
that.

1237
00:59:48,530 --> 00:59:51,590
If you can explicitly remove those, quote unquote,

1238
00:59:51,590 --> 00:59:54,910
"false negative" samples, which is basically like you have

1239
00:59:54,910 --> 00:59:58,190
golden retrievers, and you want to not tell your model that

1240
00:59:58,190 --> 01:00:01,910
another golden retriever should be really far apart.

1241
01:00:01,910 --> 01:00:04,575
And particularly, we want that far apart with a margin.

1242
01:00:04,575 --> 01:00:05,950
So it's not just saying it should

1243
01:00:05,950 --> 01:00:06,770
be a little farther apart.

1244
01:00:06,770 --> 01:00:09,230
It's really trying to push it outside of a margin far away

1245
01:00:09,230 --> 01:00:11,647
from these points, which is something we don't necessarily

1246
01:00:11,647 --> 01:00:12,270
want.

1247
01:00:12,270 --> 01:00:17,150
If you remove all of those and you use only the true negatives,

1248
01:00:17,150 --> 01:00:20,790
you actually end up getting much higher top one accuracy

1249
01:00:20,790 --> 01:00:22,190
downstream.

1250
01:00:22,190 --> 01:00:24,230
But again, this assumes that you have access

1251
01:00:24,230 --> 01:00:27,850
to labeled data for all of your data points

1252
01:00:27,850 --> 01:00:29,310
and that the labeled data probably

1253
01:00:29,310 --> 01:00:31,227
needs to align with the downstream task you're

1254
01:00:31,227 --> 01:00:32,830
interested in.

1255
01:00:32,830 --> 01:00:34,310
And so then there's a lot of people

1256
01:00:34,310 --> 01:00:37,090
who've looked at how we can leverage some supervision,

1257
01:00:37,090 --> 01:00:40,440
maybe not requiring all the points to be labeled, but maybe

1258
01:00:40,440 --> 01:00:42,600
if you have labels for some of them,

1259
01:00:42,600 --> 01:00:45,080
how we can look at supervised or even semi-supervised

1260
01:00:45,080 --> 01:00:47,200
contrastive learning.

1261
01:00:47,200 --> 01:00:51,600
And so here, the idea is that having the contrastive learning

1262
01:00:51,600 --> 01:00:53,400
signal, that triplet loss signal,

1263
01:00:53,400 --> 01:00:57,040
you get some more geometric and robust feedback than if you just

1264
01:00:57,040 --> 01:00:59,600
do crossentropy loss.

1265
01:00:59,600 --> 01:01:02,380
So then if you have images from the same class,

1266
01:01:02,380 --> 01:01:04,600
you can use those as positive pairs.

1267
01:01:04,600 --> 01:01:10,540
Or if you don't actually have labels for everything

1268
01:01:10,540 --> 01:01:12,400
but you do have labels for some things,

1269
01:01:12,400 --> 01:01:15,120
you can use those positive examples as anchors

1270
01:01:15,120 --> 01:01:20,140
and then enforce dimensions across,

1271
01:01:20,140 --> 01:01:22,720
like whether you do or don't have supervisory signal that

1272
01:01:22,720 --> 01:01:25,160
tries to get a little bit more structure

1273
01:01:25,160 --> 01:01:27,680
and handle negatives and positives reasonably.

1274
01:01:27,680 --> 01:01:31,240
And there's a lot of work along these lines.

1275
01:01:31,240 --> 01:01:32,160
Cool.

1276
01:01:32,160 --> 01:01:36,960
So representation learning using similarity

1277
01:01:36,960 --> 01:01:39,490
seems to be really powerful.

1278
01:01:39,490 --> 01:01:41,490
So just to finish up the class, I

1279
01:01:41,490 --> 01:01:46,990
want to do a bit of a case study with one particular data set.

1280
01:01:46,990 --> 01:01:49,770
This is the iNaturalist 2021 data set.

1281
01:01:49,770 --> 01:01:54,210
It has images of species, so 10,000 different species

1282
01:01:54,210 --> 01:01:57,210
of plants, animals, and wildlife, insects.

1283
01:01:57,210 --> 01:01:59,230
About 2.7 million training images--

1284
01:01:59,230 --> 01:02:03,490
so it's not on the order of billions, but it's pretty big--

1285
01:02:03,490 --> 01:02:08,370
about 50,000 validation images, and 500,000 test images.

1286
01:02:08,370 --> 01:02:12,410
And the interesting thing about this data set

1287
01:02:12,410 --> 01:02:15,470
is it has pretty explicit structure.

1288
01:02:15,470 --> 01:02:17,730
It's species, so you can look at the taxonomic tree

1289
01:02:17,730 --> 01:02:19,530
over the set of categories.

1290
01:02:19,530 --> 01:02:23,050
And here, if you start at the middle,

1291
01:02:23,050 --> 01:02:27,050
this is high-order branching in terms of taxonomic tree.

1292
01:02:27,050 --> 01:02:28,730
So it's very coarse grained.

1293
01:02:28,730 --> 01:02:32,450
This would be something like the difference between Animalia

1294
01:02:32,450 --> 01:02:33,310
and Plantae.

1295
01:02:33,310 --> 01:02:34,728
These are animals versus plants.

1296
01:02:34,728 --> 01:02:36,770
They're very, very different, and this difference

1297
01:02:36,770 --> 01:02:39,470
is very coarse-grained.

1298
01:02:39,470 --> 01:02:41,870
And then as, you move closer to the edge,

1299
01:02:41,870 --> 01:02:44,950
you would get things that are increasingly more and more

1300
01:02:44,950 --> 01:02:45,650
similar.

1301
01:02:45,650 --> 01:02:48,510
So different species within the same genera

1302
01:02:48,510 --> 01:02:50,537
are going to probably be quite visually similar,

1303
01:02:50,537 --> 01:02:52,870
though, of course, there are some exceptions from things

1304
01:02:52,870 --> 01:02:54,870
like mimicry.

1305
01:02:54,870 --> 01:02:57,390
So at the very fine edge, that's where

1306
01:02:57,390 --> 01:03:00,110
you get things like the two moths I keep showing.

1307
01:03:00,110 --> 01:03:03,190
These are different species, but from the same genus.

1308
01:03:03,190 --> 01:03:06,810
And so you can of unroll this level of granularity

1309
01:03:06,810 --> 01:03:10,190
that's-- we've got this proxy for the level of visual

1310
01:03:10,190 --> 01:03:13,350
granularity in the classes coming from the taxonomic tree.

1311
01:03:13,350 --> 01:03:15,550
So you can roll this out on a graph

1312
01:03:15,550 --> 01:03:17,850
and say, OK, kingdom is very coarse-grained.

1313
01:03:17,850 --> 01:03:20,310
Going down to species is very finegrained.

1314
01:03:20,310 --> 01:03:23,910
So one of the things that's quite interesting is if we look

1315
01:03:23,910 --> 01:03:27,850
at a supervised model versus SimCLR or MoCo,

1316
01:03:27,850 --> 01:03:32,150
these two different approaches for self-supervised contrastive

1317
01:03:32,150 --> 01:03:35,350
learning, and we look at accuracy now using a linear

1318
01:03:35,350 --> 01:03:37,120
probe--

1319
01:03:37,120 --> 01:03:39,520
so that means basically taking a representation space

1320
01:03:39,520 --> 01:03:42,200
and then just learning a linear projection that's going

1321
01:03:42,200 --> 01:03:44,202
to actually be our categorizer.

1322
01:03:44,202 --> 01:03:46,160
And we'll actually learn that linear projection

1323
01:03:46,160 --> 01:03:47,520
with supervision.

1324
01:03:47,520 --> 01:03:50,120
Then we can look at the accuracy across

1325
01:03:50,120 --> 01:03:52,120
these different granularities.

1326
01:03:52,120 --> 01:03:53,760
And so this black line up top, that's

1327
01:03:53,760 --> 01:03:55,140
supervised training from scratch.

1328
01:03:55,140 --> 01:03:57,015
We're not starting with ImageNet or anything.

1329
01:03:57,015 --> 01:03:59,680
It's just all of this is only trained on this data.

1330
01:03:59,680 --> 01:04:01,858
And then, at the bottom, we see what

1331
01:04:01,858 --> 01:04:03,400
we get from self-supervised learning,

1332
01:04:03,400 --> 01:04:05,980
again with that linear probe on the entire data set,

1333
01:04:05,980 --> 01:04:09,640
so on the whole training data set with the linear probe.

1334
01:04:09,640 --> 01:04:13,360
And what we do is we train both the supervised

1335
01:04:13,360 --> 01:04:15,880
and the self-supervised, then the linear probe

1336
01:04:15,880 --> 01:04:19,240
for the self-supervised representation on the finest

1337
01:04:19,240 --> 01:04:21,007
level, so on the species level.

1338
01:04:21,007 --> 01:04:22,840
And then instead of explicitly training them

1339
01:04:22,840 --> 01:04:25,580
for these higher-order levels, we just roll them up.

1340
01:04:25,580 --> 01:04:28,480
So you would say, OK, we're going to predict at the species

1341
01:04:28,480 --> 01:04:29,060
level.

1342
01:04:29,060 --> 01:04:31,560
Now you just aggregate all of your category predictions

1343
01:04:31,560 --> 01:04:35,130
up a level based on that graph, on that taxonomic tree.

1344
01:04:35,130 --> 01:04:37,610
You use that as your prediction for the next level,

1345
01:04:37,610 --> 01:04:39,490
and et cetera.

1346
01:04:39,490 --> 01:04:41,508
So we're basically training on the finest level,

1347
01:04:41,508 --> 01:04:43,050
but then we're evaluating our ability

1348
01:04:43,050 --> 01:04:46,410
to categorize a coarser and coarser levels.

1349
01:04:46,410 --> 01:04:49,630
And it turns out that if you look at this type of data set,

1350
01:04:49,630 --> 01:04:52,790
you can really see that, at this finegrained levels,

1351
01:04:52,790 --> 01:04:55,130
there's really not a small difference.

1352
01:04:55,130 --> 01:05:00,570
There's about a 30% gap between a supervised and self-supervised

1353
01:05:00,570 --> 01:05:02,050
training on iNaturalist.

1354
01:05:02,050 --> 01:05:05,140
And if you do the same experiment on ImageNet,

1355
01:05:05,140 --> 01:05:06,390
the gap is much, much smaller.

1356
01:05:06,390 --> 01:05:08,650
It's more like 7%.

1357
01:05:08,650 --> 01:05:11,810
And so one reason for that is this--

1358
01:05:11,810 --> 01:05:14,030
ImageNet is a very coarse grained data set.

1359
01:05:14,030 --> 01:05:16,490
The categories are not very similar to each other.

1360
01:05:16,490 --> 01:05:20,190
And you see the same performance or the same trend here.

1361
01:05:20,190 --> 01:05:21,930
The gap is very small when you look

1362
01:05:21,930 --> 01:05:25,010
at these very coarse groupings-- class, phylum, or kingdom.

1363
01:05:25,010 --> 01:05:27,890
But then the gap actually grows as the evaluation

1364
01:05:27,890 --> 01:05:29,730
is made more fine grained.

1365
01:05:29,730 --> 01:05:33,495
And so this interestingly captures

1366
01:05:33,495 --> 01:05:35,620
some of those dimensions that we were talking about

1367
01:05:35,620 --> 01:05:38,500
in terms of the ways that we have chosen our data

1368
01:05:38,500 --> 01:05:41,620
augmentations, the pairs that we give the model

1369
01:05:41,620 --> 01:05:44,860
or don't give the model, and the effect that might have.

1370
01:05:44,860 --> 01:05:48,140
So first, if we look at this and we actually

1371
01:05:48,140 --> 01:05:50,820
try to probe these representations that are learned

1372
01:05:50,820 --> 01:05:54,220
using this kind of query approach or this retrieval

1373
01:05:54,220 --> 01:05:56,860
approach, and we say, OK, we're going to start with this image.

1374
01:05:56,860 --> 01:05:59,500
Now if we take our supervised model

1375
01:05:59,500 --> 01:06:03,980
and we find the nearest neighbors in the entire data

1376
01:06:03,980 --> 01:06:08,900
set, it turns out this is a hard problem.

1377
01:06:08,900 --> 01:06:11,060
And so there are a few examples outlined

1378
01:06:11,060 --> 01:06:14,420
in blue, where we do actually get nearest neighbors that

1379
01:06:14,420 --> 01:06:16,660
are the same species.

1380
01:06:16,660 --> 01:06:19,740
But then we also get a lot of nearest neighbors

1381
01:06:19,740 --> 01:06:21,300
that are different species.

1382
01:06:21,300 --> 01:06:25,420
So even with supervision, this problem can be quite difficult.

1383
01:06:25,420 --> 01:06:28,375
And it turns out that as you aggregate up the category set,

1384
01:06:28,375 --> 01:06:30,000
no, these are not all the same species,

1385
01:06:30,000 --> 01:06:34,070
but they are in the same of genus or family.

1386
01:06:34,070 --> 01:06:39,670
But now, if you take the same sort of SimCLR representation

1387
01:06:39,670 --> 01:06:42,510
and you do the same thing, you use the same image,

1388
01:06:42,510 --> 01:06:45,630
and you query into the representation space,

1389
01:06:45,630 --> 01:06:50,350
you can see that none of them are the same species.

1390
01:06:50,350 --> 01:06:52,270
What has the model learned?

1391
01:06:52,270 --> 01:06:53,518
Yeah?

1392
01:06:53,518 --> 01:06:54,810
AUDIENCE: Oh, I had a question.

1393
01:06:54,810 --> 01:06:56,430
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

1394
01:06:56,430 --> 01:06:58,730
AUDIENCE: It relates to a couple slides back.

1395
01:06:58,730 --> 01:07:03,190
You had the accuracy of the supervised

1396
01:07:03,190 --> 01:07:05,452
and the contrastive learning.

1397
01:07:05,452 --> 01:07:06,910
So earlier in the lecture, you were

1398
01:07:06,910 --> 01:07:10,270
saying that the representation space that contrastive learning

1399
01:07:10,270 --> 01:07:11,833
learns is uniform.

1400
01:07:11,833 --> 01:07:13,250
In some sense, that's really good.

1401
01:07:13,250 --> 01:07:16,670
Whereas the supervised version won't necessarily

1402
01:07:16,670 --> 01:07:19,310
learn a uniform embedding space.

1403
01:07:19,310 --> 01:07:22,970
But you're showing that supervised is doing much better,

1404
01:07:22,970 --> 01:07:26,430
so maybe it's not that important to have a uniform embedding

1405
01:07:26,430 --> 01:07:26,930
space.

1406
01:07:26,930 --> 01:07:30,670
I'm trying to reconcile those two points.

1407
01:07:30,670 --> 01:07:34,800
SARA BEERY: Yeah, and actually, I think this will actually help.

1408
01:07:34,800 --> 01:07:39,740
So it depends a little bit on the signal.

1409
01:07:39,740 --> 01:07:45,640
So basically, yeah, it's usually good to have this separability.

1410
01:07:45,640 --> 01:07:49,140
But for contrastive learning to work well,

1411
01:07:49,140 --> 01:07:53,000
you need to have a good similarity measure

1412
01:07:53,000 --> 01:07:55,440
for your problem of interest.

1413
01:07:55,440 --> 01:07:57,680
And so here, what I'm trying to show,

1414
01:07:57,680 --> 01:08:02,160
actually, is that what we've learned is we've taken an image

1415
01:08:02,160 --> 01:08:03,820
and we've augmented it in some way,

1416
01:08:03,820 --> 01:08:06,200
and then we force those things to be close together.

1417
01:08:06,200 --> 01:08:10,320
And it looks like that the types of augmentations we're doing is

1418
01:08:10,320 --> 01:08:14,260
giving us more information about contextual similarity--

1419
01:08:14,260 --> 01:08:17,560
so, in this case, birds being held in human hands--

1420
01:08:17,560 --> 01:08:21,220
than it is giving us information about species similarity,

1421
01:08:21,220 --> 01:08:23,920
which is what we're hoping this model will be good at

1422
01:08:23,920 --> 01:08:26,200
and what we're evaluating it on.

1423
01:08:26,200 --> 01:08:30,609
So if you don't have a similarity measure,

1424
01:08:30,609 --> 01:08:33,450
where you're choosing those positive pairs

1425
01:08:33,450 --> 01:08:35,590
and saying these are similar and this is different

1426
01:08:35,590 --> 01:08:37,930
that's well aligned with your downstream goal, which

1427
01:08:37,930 --> 01:08:40,649
in this case is identifying species, then

1428
01:08:40,649 --> 01:08:43,990
these contrastive objectives, they're giving you uniformity,

1429
01:08:43,990 --> 01:08:46,890
but they're maybe not giving you the uniformity that you want,

1430
01:08:46,890 --> 01:08:51,290
or they're not giving you the similarity that you want.

1431
01:08:51,290 --> 01:08:52,529
Does that make sense?

1432
01:08:52,529 --> 01:08:56,290
AUDIENCE: So, in this case, if you were able to, say, control

1433
01:08:56,290 --> 01:08:59,970
for birds versus birds in hands, like maybe segment

1434
01:08:59,970 --> 01:09:02,210
out the birds, then do you expect

1435
01:09:02,210 --> 01:09:04,689
that accuracy to be pushed down closer

1436
01:09:04,689 --> 01:09:06,450
to the supervised accuracy?

1437
01:09:06,450 --> 01:09:09,890
SARA BEERY: Yeah, so I think the point here

1438
01:09:09,890 --> 01:09:12,569
that I'm trying to make is that if you

1439
01:09:12,569 --> 01:09:15,609
don't have a self-supervised signal that

1440
01:09:15,609 --> 01:09:20,210
is capturing the task that you actually have in mind,

1441
01:09:20,210 --> 01:09:24,450
then it's possible that the contrastive objective really

1442
01:09:24,450 --> 01:09:26,290
will not build a representation that

1443
01:09:26,290 --> 01:09:30,220
is good in the context of your downstream task.

1444
01:09:30,220 --> 01:09:34,120
Yeah, so if you did, if we had-- for example,

1445
01:09:34,120 --> 01:09:36,380
if we had a supervised objective here,

1446
01:09:36,380 --> 01:09:37,979
then you would expect it to be much

1447
01:09:37,979 --> 01:09:40,660
closer to a supervised learning objective.

1448
01:09:40,660 --> 01:09:43,939
And I actually think one of the things that I didn't do or--

1449
01:09:43,939 --> 01:09:47,420
this is some work that was led by Elijah Cole.

1450
01:09:47,420 --> 01:09:49,260
It would be interesting to look at taking

1451
01:09:49,260 --> 01:09:51,580
a contrastive objective, where now we're

1452
01:09:51,580 --> 01:09:53,163
using supervised contrastive learning

1453
01:09:53,163 --> 01:09:55,080
and see how that actually affects performance,

1454
01:09:55,080 --> 01:09:56,622
though I actually wouldn't anticipate

1455
01:09:56,622 --> 01:10:01,020
it would be so different from crossentropy loss in this case.

1456
01:10:01,020 --> 01:10:04,100
So I think this is interesting because it kind of points out

1457
01:10:04,100 --> 01:10:07,300
how the benchmarks that we use really

1458
01:10:07,300 --> 01:10:10,980
influence our idea of what's good enough because it turns out

1459
01:10:10,980 --> 01:10:13,500
that, with ImageNet, we're saying, wow,

1460
01:10:13,500 --> 01:10:16,660
without any labels at all, we're still building representations

1461
01:10:16,660 --> 01:10:18,940
that are very, very close to as good as supervised

1462
01:10:18,940 --> 01:10:20,160
representations.

1463
01:10:20,160 --> 01:10:21,900
But it turns out that that's, again,

1464
01:10:21,900 --> 01:10:23,540
contextualized on the task of interest.

1465
01:10:23,540 --> 01:10:25,820
With ImageNet, our task is coarse-grained.

1466
01:10:25,820 --> 01:10:28,950
And it turns out, the augmentations were building in,

1467
01:10:28,950 --> 01:10:30,950
they're capturing things that make

1468
01:10:30,950 --> 01:10:33,735
it easy to separate coarse grained visual characteristics.

1469
01:10:33,735 --> 01:10:35,110
Maybe they're relying on the fact

1470
01:10:35,110 --> 01:10:37,950
that, at a coarse-grained level, all those birds on hands

1471
01:10:37,950 --> 01:10:38,897
would be put together.

1472
01:10:38,897 --> 01:10:40,730
That's fine because we just care about bird.

1473
01:10:40,730 --> 01:10:42,470
We don't care about bird categories.

1474
01:10:42,470 --> 01:10:45,430
But as soon as you have some of these categorizations that

1475
01:10:45,430 --> 01:10:49,470
need to be much more subtle, then the augmentations

1476
01:10:49,470 --> 01:10:53,950
would somehow need to capture that well.

1477
01:10:53,950 --> 01:10:55,730
Yeah, any other questions about this?

1478
01:10:59,230 --> 01:11:00,230
Yeah?

1479
01:11:00,230 --> 01:11:02,430
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

1480
01:11:07,910 --> 01:11:10,750
SARA BEERY: So the question was, does SimCLR or does--

1481
01:11:10,750 --> 01:11:12,790
I mean, I think SimCLR is just one

1482
01:11:12,790 --> 01:11:16,390
example of a self-supervised contrastive objective.

1483
01:11:16,390 --> 01:11:20,190
Does that usually do bad on unseen data?

1484
01:11:20,190 --> 01:11:25,700
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] so you don't know what it is?

1485
01:11:28,600 --> 01:11:31,360
SARA BEERY: Sorry, can you try to repeat that one more time.

1486
01:11:31,360 --> 01:11:34,460
AUDIENCE: So in the negative samples,

1487
01:11:34,460 --> 01:11:36,060
we are not doing any augmentations.

1488
01:11:36,060 --> 01:11:37,560
We are not doing anything.

1489
01:11:37,560 --> 01:11:39,360
So I'm just trying to understand how

1490
01:11:39,360 --> 01:11:42,200
the model sees the negative samples [INAUDIBLE]

1491
01:11:42,200 --> 01:11:43,820
not doing anything on it.

1492
01:11:43,820 --> 01:11:50,760
So when it sees something which is not seen, how does it act?

1493
01:11:50,760 --> 01:11:54,980
SARA BEERY: So, I mean, in a lot of these cases,

1494
01:11:54,980 --> 01:11:56,600
what we're actually showing results

1495
01:11:56,600 --> 01:12:00,000
on is test data, which is unseen during training.

1496
01:12:00,000 --> 01:12:02,965
So what we are actually showing that these things do

1497
01:12:02,965 --> 01:12:04,840
for some things, like ImageNet in particular,

1498
01:12:04,840 --> 01:12:07,560
do generalize well to unseen data.

1499
01:12:07,560 --> 01:12:10,125
But the point you brought up about augmentations

1500
01:12:10,125 --> 01:12:12,500
on the negative examples-- and it is an interesting one--

1501
01:12:12,500 --> 01:12:16,320
and I think, actually, one of the things that is kind of funny

1502
01:12:16,320 --> 01:12:19,498
is if you only did augmentations on the positive samples,

1503
01:12:19,498 --> 01:12:22,040
you never do any augmentations on the negative samples, which

1504
01:12:22,040 --> 01:12:24,613
in practice is not necessarily what people are doing,

1505
01:12:24,613 --> 01:12:26,780
you can imagine that the model might learn something

1506
01:12:26,780 --> 01:12:30,540
about statistic weirdness in terms of the input space

1507
01:12:30,540 --> 01:12:34,140
versus not, like more natural statistics, and start picking up

1508
01:12:34,140 --> 01:12:35,300
on that signal.

1509
01:12:35,300 --> 01:12:38,100
So that is an interesting bit of intuition you brought up.

1510
01:12:38,100 --> 01:12:41,740
But no, generally, I think, actually,

1511
01:12:41,740 --> 01:12:43,960
because we're showing this on unseen test data,

1512
01:12:43,960 --> 01:12:46,120
I think there is something reasonable about that.

1513
01:12:46,120 --> 01:12:52,620
And we've actually found that these types

1514
01:12:52,620 --> 01:12:55,500
of contrastive objectives, or the triplet loss objectives,

1515
01:12:55,500 --> 01:12:58,220
often will generalize better.

1516
01:12:58,220 --> 01:13:00,380
So the example, I'm specifically going to bring up

1517
01:13:00,380 --> 01:13:04,140
is this idea of individual re-identification.

1518
01:13:04,140 --> 01:13:09,200
In almost all, for example, like Celeb A,

1519
01:13:09,200 --> 01:13:11,700
this recognizing celebrities again,

1520
01:13:11,700 --> 01:13:14,220
almost all the best methods on this type

1521
01:13:14,220 --> 01:13:16,660
of individual identification, where you explicitly

1522
01:13:16,660 --> 01:13:18,500
want to be able to say, even if it's not

1523
01:13:18,500 --> 01:13:21,420
a category you've seen before-- so, for example, a human that

1524
01:13:21,420 --> 01:13:23,470
hasn't been seen by the model-- you

1525
01:13:23,470 --> 01:13:25,950
want a picture of that human and another picture

1526
01:13:25,950 --> 01:13:28,210
of that new human to be close together.

1527
01:13:28,210 --> 01:13:30,673
And it turns out that these metric spaces, particularly

1528
01:13:30,673 --> 01:13:32,590
learned through contrastive objectives, things

1529
01:13:32,590 --> 01:13:34,190
like triplet loss and then building up

1530
01:13:34,190 --> 01:13:37,170
to more and more complex contrastive objectives,

1531
01:13:37,170 --> 01:13:39,470
those explicitly are better at that open set

1532
01:13:39,470 --> 01:13:46,350
clustering problem in a way that actually a standard crossentropy

1533
01:13:46,350 --> 01:13:48,750
loss based on a fixed number of categories,

1534
01:13:48,750 --> 01:13:50,630
there's almost nothing you can reasonably

1535
01:13:50,630 --> 01:13:54,410
say about what will happen when you look at new categories.

1536
01:13:54,410 --> 01:13:58,510
And in practice, actually, similarly to this, what we find

1537
01:13:58,510 --> 01:14:01,990
is that if you use classification losses

1538
01:14:01,990 --> 01:14:05,390
versus contrastive losses, the contrastive losses significantly

1539
01:14:05,390 --> 01:14:09,110
benefit for that open set in terms of making

1540
01:14:09,110 --> 01:14:11,430
things similar close together.

1541
01:14:11,430 --> 01:14:13,830
AUDIENCE: I guess the reason I got confused initially

1542
01:14:13,830 --> 01:14:15,750
[INAUDIBLE] discussed in the lecture

1543
01:14:15,750 --> 01:14:18,836
that for positive samples [INAUDIBLE],

1544
01:14:18,836 --> 01:14:23,040
but for the negative samples, we just randomly pick any images.

1545
01:14:23,040 --> 01:14:25,100
So I thought we were not doing augmentations,

1546
01:14:25,100 --> 01:14:26,880
and it looks like we are.

1547
01:14:26,880 --> 01:14:29,580
SARA BEERY: I mean, not everything does.

1548
01:14:29,580 --> 01:14:32,120
But yes, there are cases where people do augmentations

1549
01:14:32,120 --> 01:14:33,140
on negative examples.

1550
01:14:33,140 --> 01:14:35,920
Yeah, but I think that is interesting.

1551
01:14:35,920 --> 01:14:37,020
Any other questions?

1552
01:14:40,600 --> 01:14:41,840
Cool.

1553
01:14:41,840 --> 01:14:44,800
So yeah, to summarize again, we tried

1554
01:14:44,800 --> 01:14:46,520
to capture some dimensions of what

1555
01:14:46,520 --> 01:14:50,400
makes a representation good, and specifically

1556
01:14:50,400 --> 01:14:52,480
that good representations might actually

1557
01:14:52,480 --> 01:14:56,280
be really based on similarity and dissimilarity.

1558
01:14:56,280 --> 01:14:57,680
We said that we want to make sure

1559
01:14:57,680 --> 01:15:01,240
that representations are well clustered, that they're compact,

1560
01:15:01,240 --> 01:15:04,600
and that they're separated, so the classes are spread out.

1561
01:15:04,600 --> 01:15:06,820
We want to preserve relevant information,

1562
01:15:06,820 --> 01:15:09,620
and we want to teach relevant invariances.

1563
01:15:09,620 --> 01:15:11,400
We want the model to almost forget

1564
01:15:11,400 --> 01:15:13,220
the irrelevant information.

1565
01:15:13,220 --> 01:15:15,880
Where here, we're explicitly defining what's irrelevant

1566
01:15:15,880 --> 01:15:19,200
based on, for example, in those self-supervised objectives,

1567
01:15:19,200 --> 01:15:22,210
showing different augmentations that we want the model to be

1568
01:15:22,210 --> 01:15:24,870
invariant to, and in the supervised case,

1569
01:15:24,870 --> 01:15:26,730
what we're saying is, OK, we want the model

1570
01:15:26,730 --> 01:15:28,610
to be invariant to the things that

1571
01:15:28,610 --> 01:15:31,430
are different about these two images of the same category.

1572
01:15:31,430 --> 01:15:34,350
So that might, for example, be the context,

1573
01:15:34,350 --> 01:15:37,770
the fact that the bird is being held in a hand.

1574
01:15:37,770 --> 01:15:39,890
And then we can do this in a supervised

1575
01:15:39,890 --> 01:15:42,490
or a self-supervised way.

1576
01:15:42,490 --> 01:15:44,290
And it all comes down to the difference

1577
01:15:44,290 --> 01:15:46,290
between supervised and self-supervised,

1578
01:15:46,290 --> 01:15:49,530
the way that we decide what's a similar pair.

1579
01:15:49,530 --> 01:15:51,650
And so we can actually build a lot of knowledge

1580
01:15:51,650 --> 01:15:54,030
into the systems around what should be similar

1581
01:15:54,030 --> 01:15:56,970
and what should be different based on our downstream goals.

1582
01:15:56,970 --> 01:15:57,530
Cool.

